WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.980 align:middle line:90%


00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:02.940 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:04.410 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:07.350 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:17.460 align:middle line:90%


00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:20.760 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: OK, let me
go ahead and get started.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:26.460 align:middle line:84%
So today I'm starting my
week on static competition.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:29.670 align:middle line:84%
So think about
what's going on here.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:33.190 align:middle line:84%
So far, the first two weeks have
mostly been monopoly models.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:38.490 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, most industries
or most things of interest

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:39.940 align:middle line:90%
have multiple firms in it.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:41.850 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to now
start every lecture

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:43.350 align:middle line:84%
for the rest of the
term, it's going

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:46.440 align:middle line:90%
to be multiple firms competing.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:48.640 align:middle line:84%
When Tirole goes
through his book,

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:51.870 align:middle line:84%
he does this long-run
to short-run chart

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:56.040 align:middle line:84%
where he talks about in the
short-run firms can basically

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.900 align:middle line:84%
do nothing in competing with
each other than setting prices.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:01.740 align:middle line:84%
And then you get a sequence
of variables that take

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:03.310 align:middle line:90%
longer and longer to adjust.

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:07.115 align:middle line:84%
And I think the way his book
is written is, in some sense,

00:01:07.115 --> 00:01:08.490 align:middle line:84%
the way we solve
models is always

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:11.233 align:middle line:84%
by backward-- like multi-stage
games by backward induction.

00:01:11.233 --> 00:01:13.900 align:middle line:84%
You solve the last stage and the
next to last stage and the next

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:14.860 align:middle line:90%
to last stage.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:17.380 align:middle line:84%
And so his book is
written this way, where

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:18.640 align:middle line:90%
you go backward from here.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:22.602 align:middle line:84%
And that's how my
course is written.

00:01:22.602 --> 00:01:24.310 align:middle line:84%
Although, these days
it's really going to

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:27.790 align:middle line:84%
be an awful lot of discussion
of this and then other things

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:30.970 align:middle line:90%
just as individual topics.

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:35.770 align:middle line:84%
So the next few weeks, it's
going to be short-run things.

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:37.840 align:middle line:84%
When we're discussing
short-run competition,

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:38.870 align:middle line:90%
what do we talk about?

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:42.830 align:middle line:84%
One of the big things we
talk about is markups.

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:44.890 align:middle line:84%
We talk about markups
a lot because markups

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:46.130 align:middle line:90%
lead to deadweight loss.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:48.550 align:middle line:90%
That's a major welfare concern.

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:50.825 align:middle line:84%
But they're also
going to be, just

00:01:50.825 --> 00:01:53.200 align:middle line:84%
like in price discrimination,
you get that second welfare

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:57.650 align:middle line:84%
concern of the misallocation of
the goods that are being sold.

00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:00.710 align:middle line:84%
We'll also get
mispurchasing of goods

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:02.910 align:middle line:84%
that becomes another
consideration here as well.

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:04.660 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm going to
do in this lecture is

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:07.420 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to start with all
of the classic stuff in I/O

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:09.880 align:middle line:84%
that people don't do
anymore but do that as,

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:11.920 align:middle line:84%
I think, background
for things that

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:15.880 align:middle line:84%
is how modern studies
of competition are done.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:18.240 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, I start--
and I'm talking

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:19.480 align:middle line:90%
about old historical stuff.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:24.860 align:middle line:84%
And I added some pictures
of the old, dead white guys

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:26.100 align:middle line:90%
who developed these things.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:32.090 align:middle line:84%
So there's Cournot,
Cournot competition 1838.

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:34.073 align:middle line:84%
So obviously,
Cournot was thinking

00:02:34.073 --> 00:02:35.990 align:middle line:84%
about a world that's
very different from ours,

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:38.900 align:middle line:84%
a world in which many of the
things that people bought

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:44.330 align:middle line:84%
were completely undifferentiated
goods, like wheat and corn

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:48.650 align:middle line:84%
and wool, or whatever, where
just a product is a product.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:51.060 align:middle line:84%
And there's a universal
market for it.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:54.217 align:middle line:84%
So Cournot thought about a
model where you had n firms.

00:02:54.217 --> 00:02:56.300 align:middle line:84%
There was some homogeneous
good that they were all

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:57.950 align:middle line:84%
producing that
had inverse demand

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:02.900 align:middle line:84%
P of X. The producers have
cost functions, ci of xi that

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:09.920 align:middle line:84%
depend on what is the
quantity produced by Firm I.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:12.870 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, Cournot
invented Nash equilibrium.

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:14.380 align:middle line:90%
So Cournot thought of a model.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:16.640 align:middle line:84%
He had this timing where
the firms are simultaneously

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:19.755 align:middle line:90%
choosing outputs x1 through xn.

00:03:19.755 --> 00:03:21.380 align:middle line:84%
When they choose
those outputs, they're

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:23.740 align:middle line:84%
going to all bring their
goods to the market.

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:26.300 align:middle line:84%
There's going to be a
market-determined price.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:31.220 align:middle line:84%
And all the firms are
choosing their quantity,

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:33.710 align:middle line:84%
assuming that the quantities
chosen by the other firms

00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:34.730 align:middle line:90%
are all held fixed.

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:37.700 align:middle line:84%
And that's going to be the
optimality condition, OK?

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:40.790 align:middle line:90%


00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:43.170 align:middle line:84%
OK, what happens in
Cournot competition?

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:46.790 align:middle line:84%
So in Cournot
competition, the firm

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:57.580 align:middle line:84%
is maximizing over
xi, xi times P of xi

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:06.400 align:middle line:84%
plus sum over j different
from i xj minus ci of xi.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:09.880 align:middle line:84%
So that's every firm's
maximization problem,

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:14.230 align:middle line:84%
is maximize quantity times the
market price minus the cost.

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:18.100 align:middle line:84%
Your quantity affects
how much you produce.

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:20.320 align:middle line:84%
It ends up affecting
the market price

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:23.890 align:middle line:84%
through its impact on
the aggregate supply.

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:28.300 align:middle line:84%
And it obviously affects
your cost of production.

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:29.930 align:middle line:90%
So what do we get?

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:32.120 align:middle line:84%
Well, if a profile is
a Nash equilibrium,

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:37.580 align:middle line:84%
it's got to satisfy first-order
condition for this for each i.

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:39.735 align:middle line:84%
What does the first-order
condition look like?

00:04:39.735 --> 00:04:42.850 align:middle line:90%


00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:46.180 align:middle line:84%
So first, it's this times the
derivative of this gives you,

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:50.200 align:middle line:84%
you get a P of the sum of
the xi's minus the derivative

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:52.130 align:middle line:90%
of this is marginal cost.

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:59.150 align:middle line:84%
The third term in the thing is
xi times the derivative of that.

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:04.450 align:middle line:84%
So we get Pi prime
times xi star equals 0.

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:07.690 align:middle line:84%
So this is what the
classic Cournot first-order

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:08.730 align:middle line:90%
condition looks like.

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:15.080 align:middle line:90%


00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:16.980 align:middle line:90%
What are some implications?

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:22.640 align:middle line:84%
So first of all, price ends
up greater than marginal cost.

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:26.840 align:middle line:84%
This is price minus marginal
cost is the first term.

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:29.940 align:middle line:84%
If xi is a positive number,
P prime is a negative number.

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:33.710 align:middle line:84%
So this is price minus
marginal cost equals minus Pi

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:37.220 align:middle line:90%
prime xi is a positive number.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:38.690 align:middle line:90%
Production in a Cournot--

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:41.460 align:middle line:84%
so we get a second welfare
loss in a Cournot oligopoly.

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:44.610 align:middle line:84%
So the first welfare loss
is there's deadweight loss.

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:47.310 align:middle line:84%
Second is production is
inefficient in a Cournot

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:48.530 align:middle line:90%
oligopoly.

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:52.100 align:middle line:84%
Production is inefficient
in an asymmetric oligopoly

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:55.910 align:middle line:84%
because if the firms are
asymmetric in equilibrium,

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:58.250 align:middle line:84%
x1 is going to be
different from x2.

00:05:58.250 --> 00:06:02.420 align:middle line:84%
If x1 is different from x2 and P
prime is the same for everyone,

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:04.670 align:middle line:84%
because it's evaluated
at the aggregate demand,

00:06:04.670 --> 00:06:07.290 align:middle line:84%
then that means-- then the
P is the same for everyone.

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:10.490 align:middle line:84%
That means the c primes
have to be different.

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:15.680 align:middle line:84%
So in an asymmetric
Cournot oligopoly,

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:18.548 align:middle line:84%
the realized marginal
costs are different.

00:06:18.548 --> 00:06:20.090 align:middle line:84%
So the quantity
that's being produced

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:23.110 align:middle line:90%
is being produced inefficiently.

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:25.750 align:middle line:84%
OK, example to think
about this is for--

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:29.000 align:middle line:90%


00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:32.270 align:middle line:84%
if you think about the model for
producing oil in the world today

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:34.050 align:middle line:84%
is something like a
Cournot oligopoly.

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:35.180 align:middle line:90%
There's a set of countries.

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:38.210 align:middle line:84%
Every country, the ones
we're centrally producing

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:41.380 align:middle line:84%
are choosing how
much to produce.

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:44.390 align:middle line:84%
If each country is
maximizing its production,

00:06:44.390 --> 00:06:46.040 align:middle line:84%
then you're going
to have countries

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:48.440 align:middle line:84%
like Saudi Arabia has oil
that they could pump out

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:51.050 align:middle line:84%
of the ground for $5 a
barrel or $10 a barrel

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:53.010 align:middle line:84%
that they're not pumping
it out of the ground.

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:56.910 align:middle line:84%
Canada is getting oil out of
tar sands for $50 a barrel.

00:06:56.910 --> 00:07:01.190 align:middle line:84%
And there's an inefficiency
there from low-cost producers

00:07:01.190 --> 00:07:03.740 align:middle line:84%
not producing, while high-cost
producers are producing,

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:06.260 align:middle line:90%
holding the quantity fixed.

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:10.860 align:middle line:84%
OK, firm outputs are usually
strategic substitutes.

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:15.380 align:middle line:84%
So if you look at a Cournot
model, if I put q1 on this axis

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:20.690 align:middle line:84%
and do what's the best response
for Firm 2 to an output of q1,

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:24.230 align:middle line:84%
you get this
downward-sloping line.

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:26.790 align:middle line:84%
And the equilibrium
looks like this.

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:33.680 align:middle line:84%
If I put q2 on this axis
and graph BR1 of q2,

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:37.190 align:middle line:84%
Cournot equilibrium is
typically this intersection

00:07:37.190 --> 00:07:40.590 align:middle line:90%
of two downward-sloping lines.

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:43.620 align:middle line:84%
OK, that's what it
normally looks like.

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:45.398 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't have
to look like that.

00:07:45.398 --> 00:07:47.440 align:middle line:84%
And the Cournot equilibrium
is not always unique.

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:49.560 align:middle line:84%
You could imagine
if you could somehow

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:51.570 align:middle line:84%
draw these things
with wiggles in them

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:55.370 align:middle line:84%
so that they intersect
multiple times.

00:07:55.370 --> 00:08:02.200 align:middle line:84%
OK, fourth result of this,
take this expression, price

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:07.330 align:middle line:84%
minus cost is minus xi
star P prime, and then

00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:09.940 align:middle line:90%
divide both sides by price.

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:13.960 align:middle line:84%
I get an expression that looks
very much like the monopoly

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:16.630 align:middle line:84%
pricing expression,
except it has

00:08:16.630 --> 00:08:19.270 align:middle line:84%
an xi star on the right-hand
side instead of having

00:08:19.270 --> 00:08:21.590 align:middle line:90%
an x star on the right side.

00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:24.320 align:middle line:84%
But in some sense this is the
monopoly first-order condition.

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:26.770 align:middle line:84%
So because this is the
monopoly first-order condition,

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:29.410 align:middle line:84%
except with an xi here
instead of a capital X

00:08:29.410 --> 00:08:33.130 align:middle line:84%
here, I get this
expression, price

00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:37.419 align:middle line:84%
minus marginal cost over
price is minus xi over x star

00:08:37.419 --> 00:08:39.280 align:middle line:90%
times 1 over epsilon.

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:43.390 align:middle line:84%
So if we think about how does
competition reduce markups

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:46.930 align:middle line:84%
in the Cournot model,
obviously, elasticities--

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:48.880 align:middle line:84%
if you don't have a
constant elasticity demand

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:51.970 align:middle line:84%
curve, elasticities also
change as output changes,

00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:54.510 align:middle line:84%
because the elasticity
is nonconstant.

00:08:54.510 --> 00:08:58.090 align:middle line:84%
But if you think about the
constant elasticity intuition,

00:08:58.090 --> 00:09:00.880 align:middle line:84%
this is going to say
that, in N firm oligopoly,

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:04.060 align:middle line:84%
prices are going to go
down roughly like 1 over n.

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:05.830 align:middle line:84%
And this is one of
the features that

00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:08.800 align:middle line:84%
made Cournot popular is that
people have this intuition.

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:10.910 align:middle line:90%
With monopoly, prices are high.

00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:14.140 align:middle line:84%
As you get more competition
in, prices ought to decline,

00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:17.290 align:middle line:84%
and they ought to decline
in some decreasing

00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:18.910 align:middle line:90%
returns to competition way.

00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:21.550 align:middle line:84%
And they ought to asymptote
to perfect competition

00:09:21.550 --> 00:09:23.920 align:middle line:84%
as the number of firms
goes to infinity.

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:25.730 align:middle line:84%
And Cournot has
those properties,

00:09:25.730 --> 00:09:27.640 align:middle line:84%
and that's one of
the reasons why it

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:29.220 align:middle line:90%
became a popular model to use.

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:32.530 align:middle line:90%


00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:36.490 align:middle line:84%
OK, notice that,
like marginal costs

00:09:36.490 --> 00:09:38.840 align:middle line:84%
are different with
asymmetric firms,

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:41.500 align:middle line:84%
Lerner indexes are different
with asymmetric firms

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:46.060 align:middle line:84%
because each firm's
markup is xi over x star.

00:09:46.060 --> 00:09:48.980 align:middle line:84%
And so the xi's are not all
1 over n at equilibrium.

00:09:48.980 --> 00:09:51.040 align:middle line:90%
The markups are different.

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:54.310 align:middle line:84%
So we can think about
what is the average markup

00:09:54.310 --> 00:09:56.390 align:middle line:90%
in an industry across firms?

00:09:56.390 --> 00:10:00.130 align:middle line:84%
So let's define the
industry-wide Lerner

00:10:00.130 --> 00:10:06.100 align:middle line:84%
index to be price minus average
marginal cost over price,

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:10.360 align:middle line:84%
where we take a weighted
average of the marginal costs,

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:13.580 align:middle line:90%
giving weight xi over x star.

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:16.330 align:middle line:84%
So we give more weight to the
firms that are producing more.

00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:21.070 align:middle line:84%
So this is price minus average
marginal cost over price.

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:23.890 align:middle line:84%
Theorem is that--
and you can just--

00:10:23.890 --> 00:10:26.560 align:middle line:84%
this is just basically taking
a weighted average of these,

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:27.880 align:middle line:90%
weighting them by xi.

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:34.310 align:middle line:84%
So the theorem is that the
industry-average Lerner index

00:10:34.310 --> 00:10:39.710 align:middle line:84%
is negative H over epsilon,
where H is the sum of xi

00:10:39.710 --> 00:10:42.590 align:middle line:90%
over x star squared.

00:10:42.590 --> 00:10:46.310 align:middle line:84%
OK, so H is the sum of
the squared market shares.

00:10:46.310 --> 00:10:50.960 align:middle line:84%
And so the Cournot
model predicts that--

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:53.480 align:middle line:90%


00:10:53.480 --> 00:10:57.800 align:middle line:84%
the Cournot model predicts
that the markup in an industry

00:10:57.800 --> 00:11:01.700 align:middle line:84%
depends on sum of
squared market shares.

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:04.970 align:middle line:84%
And sum of squared markup
shares is a summary statistic

00:11:04.970 --> 00:11:06.300 align:middle line:90%
for how large markups are.

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:11.150 align:middle line:84%
And this was sufficiently
influential that if you go back

00:11:11.150 --> 00:11:14.060 align:middle line:84%
and read how US merger
guidelines were designed

00:11:14.060 --> 00:11:18.950 align:middle line:84%
in the 1950s, they explicitly
refer to Herfindahl indexes.

00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:21.590 align:middle line:84%
And they're explicitly
referring to Herfindahl indexes

00:11:21.590 --> 00:11:24.335 align:middle line:90%
in the sense of like if--

00:11:24.335 --> 00:11:26.960 align:middle line:84%
I forget what the numbers are,
but they're something like this.

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:30.740 align:middle line:84%
If the Herfindahl index in an
industry is less than 0.18,

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:32.790 align:middle line:90%
then we will allow mergers.

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:38.330 align:middle line:84%
And if the Herfindahl index
is between 0.18 and 0.28

00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:41.720 align:middle line:90%
and the delta H--

00:11:41.720 --> 00:11:43.880 align:middle line:84%
then what we'll do is
look at whether delta H is

00:11:43.880 --> 00:11:47.120 align:middle line:90%
less than or greater than 0.01.

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:50.525 align:middle line:84%
And they will tend to consider
mergers as questionable

00:11:50.525 --> 00:11:53.150 align:middle line:84%
or not, depending on whether the
change in the Herfindahl index

00:11:53.150 --> 00:11:55.247 align:middle line:90%
is bigger or smaller than 0.01.

00:11:55.247 --> 00:11:57.830 align:middle line:84%
And then, for industries where
the Herfindahl index is already

00:11:57.830 --> 00:12:02.040 align:middle line:84%
above 0.28, then they're
going to be-- then

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:04.040 align:middle line:84%
they're going to be--
then mergers are typically

00:12:04.040 --> 00:12:05.340 align:middle line:90%
not going to be allowed.

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:09.050 align:middle line:84%
But this is just-- because
the Herfindahl index

00:12:09.050 --> 00:12:10.640 align:middle line:84%
had this role in
the theorem that it

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:13.530 align:middle line:84%
was a measure of how
competitive an industry was,

00:12:13.530 --> 00:12:16.620 align:middle line:84%
it took this very central
role in competition.

00:12:16.620 --> 00:12:18.170 align:middle line:90%
And if you look at the--

00:12:18.170 --> 00:12:23.370 align:middle line:84%
I discussed the industry cross
section I/O from the 1960s,

00:12:23.370 --> 00:12:26.552 align:middle line:84%
constant regressions of
mark-ups on Herfindahl indexes

00:12:26.552 --> 00:12:29.010 align:middle line:84%
because the Herfindahl index
was supposed to be the correct

00:12:29.010 --> 00:12:32.340 align:middle line:84%
measure of how concentrated
an industry was.

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:35.310 align:middle line:84%
One thing to note,
though, you definitely

00:12:35.310 --> 00:12:38.730 align:middle line:84%
should not be thinking of the
Herfindahl index as a welfare

00:12:38.730 --> 00:12:41.130 align:middle line:84%
proxy because you
think about it,

00:12:41.130 --> 00:12:44.250 align:middle line:84%
it's measuring mark-ups relative
to the cost they're there.

00:12:44.250 --> 00:12:46.020 align:middle line:84%
So, for instance, if
you have two firms

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:49.500 align:middle line:84%
that are perfectly symmetric,
the Herfindahl index is 0.5.

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:52.620 align:middle line:84%
It's 1/2 squared plus
1/2 squared is 0.5.

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:54.930 align:middle line:84%
Suppose one of those
firms gets more efficient

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:56.650 align:middle line:90%
and lowers its cost.

00:12:56.650 --> 00:13:00.990 align:middle line:84%
When it lowers its cost,
everyone is better off.

00:13:00.990 --> 00:13:04.170 align:middle line:84%
Social welfare goes up, but
the Herfindahl index also

00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:06.060 align:middle line:84%
goes up because the
firm with a lower cost

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:09.365 align:middle line:84%
now starts producing a
higher share of the output.

00:13:09.365 --> 00:13:14.100 align:middle line:90%


00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:17.310 align:middle line:90%
In the old days, one could--

00:13:17.310 --> 00:13:20.160 align:middle line:84%
and let me say, I think
big mistake students still

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:22.290 align:middle line:84%
make today is trying
to be too original.

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:23.790 align:middle line:84%
I think almost
the best way to do

00:13:23.790 --> 00:13:25.707 align:middle line:84%
research, especially
when you're starting out,

00:13:25.707 --> 00:13:28.030 align:middle line:84%
is always to take some existing
paper and improve on it

00:13:28.030 --> 00:13:31.038 align:middle line:84%
because if you take an existing
paper, you improve on it.

00:13:31.038 --> 00:13:33.580 align:middle line:84%
You've got something better than
an existing published paper.

00:13:33.580 --> 00:13:36.900 align:middle line:84%
Whereas, you start from scratch
with some creative idea.

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:37.920 align:middle line:90%
You spend years on it.

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:40.337 align:middle line:84%
And then you realize two years
later that what you've done

00:13:40.337 --> 00:13:43.000 align:middle line:84%
has created a worse version of
something that already exists.

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:46.050 align:middle line:84%
Whereas, if you start with
the existing frontier paper,

00:13:46.050 --> 00:13:47.560 align:middle line:90%
you know where the frontier is.

00:13:47.560 --> 00:13:48.370 align:middle line:90%
You improve on it.

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:50.750 align:middle line:84%
You've got a new
frontier paper, OK?

00:13:50.750 --> 00:13:55.470 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, back in the
day, Cournot was 1838.

00:13:55.470 --> 00:13:59.220 align:middle line:84%
Bertrand, 45 years later, picks
up Cournot's paper and says,

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:02.040 align:middle line:84%
I can come up with an
interesting comment on Cournot.

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:07.410 align:middle line:84%
So his comment on Cournot
was that many firms

00:14:07.410 --> 00:14:10.720 align:middle line:84%
don't choose the quantity
that they're going to produce.

00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:14.020 align:middle line:84%
What they do is instead set
a price for their product

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:16.750 align:middle line:84%
and then sell however
much at that price

00:14:16.750 --> 00:14:19.780 align:middle line:90%
as people are willing to buy.

00:14:19.780 --> 00:14:22.690 align:middle line:84%
So you just flipped
around this discussion

00:14:22.690 --> 00:14:25.960 align:middle line:84%
from firms choosing quantities,
treating everyone's quantity

00:14:25.960 --> 00:14:28.190 align:middle line:84%
as fixed, to firms
choosing prices.

00:14:28.190 --> 00:14:31.150 align:middle line:84%
And his comment is
basically that predictions

00:14:31.150 --> 00:14:33.440 align:middle line:84%
of Cournot's model
change dramatically

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:35.110 align:middle line:84%
if you make that
simple flip from firms

00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:37.763 align:middle line:84%
are choosing quantities,
assuming quantities

00:14:37.763 --> 00:14:39.430 align:middle line:84%
are fixed, to firms
are choosing prices,

00:14:39.430 --> 00:14:41.320 align:middle line:90%
assuming prices are fixed.

00:14:41.320 --> 00:14:46.570 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, Bertrand's model, let
x be a market demand function.

00:14:46.570 --> 00:14:49.450 align:middle line:84%
Assume that x is
weakly decreasing

00:14:49.450 --> 00:14:52.810 align:middle line:84%
and that P times
x of P is bounded.

00:14:52.810 --> 00:14:56.260 align:middle line:84%
Assume that all firms
have unit cost c.

00:14:56.260 --> 00:14:58.280 align:middle line:84%
Firms simultaneously
announce prices.

00:14:58.280 --> 00:15:00.760 align:middle line:84%
All demand goes to the
lowest price firms.

00:15:00.760 --> 00:15:05.420 align:middle line:84%
Then, we don't get this
Cournot continuity.

00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:07.270 align:middle line:84%
We get an immediate
discontinuity,

00:15:07.270 --> 00:15:10.130 align:middle line:84%
which is two firms is enough
for perfect competition.

00:15:10.130 --> 00:15:14.080 align:middle line:84%
So the unique equilibrium
of this model is P1 star

00:15:14.080 --> 00:15:17.960 align:middle line:90%
equals P2 star equals c.

00:15:17.960 --> 00:15:20.630 align:middle line:84%
And this is what Frank
Fisher and his writing

00:15:20.630 --> 00:15:23.040 align:middle line:90%
about economic theory would say.

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:24.810 align:middle line:90%
This is an exemplifying theory.

00:15:24.810 --> 00:15:28.420 align:middle line:84%
You make these somewhat
extreme assumptions,

00:15:28.420 --> 00:15:31.313 align:middle line:84%
that is, firms announced
prices, and all demand

00:15:31.313 --> 00:15:33.730 align:middle line:84%
goes to the lowest price firm,
even if it's one penny less

00:15:33.730 --> 00:15:34.610 align:middle line:90%
than the other firm.

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:37.810 align:middle line:84%
But what you show is that,
with these assumptions,

00:15:37.810 --> 00:15:40.150 align:middle line:84%
you get of extreme
outcome, which

00:15:40.150 --> 00:15:42.340 align:middle line:84%
is competition works
differently from Cournot,

00:15:42.340 --> 00:15:43.812 align:middle line:90%
and it's a discontinuous thing.

00:15:43.812 --> 00:15:45.520 align:middle line:84%
And any number of
firms is enough to have

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:46.660 align:middle line:90%
perfect competition.

00:15:46.660 --> 00:15:49.180 align:middle line:90%


00:15:49.180 --> 00:15:52.630 align:middle line:84%
One version of this model
that still does get used

00:15:52.630 --> 00:15:55.960 align:middle line:84%
is if you think about firms
that have asymmetric costs,

00:15:55.960 --> 00:15:59.420 align:middle line:84%
you have two firms with
cost c1 star and c2 star.

00:15:59.420 --> 00:16:01.360 align:middle line:90%
What's the equilibrium?

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:05.380 align:middle line:84%
The equilibrium is the firms
end up both pricing at c2 star

00:16:05.380 --> 00:16:08.760 align:middle line:84%
with all of the demand going to
the firm with the lower cost.

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:10.510 align:middle line:84%
If you want, you can
think of it as Firm 1

00:16:10.510 --> 00:16:14.080 align:middle line:84%
is charging c2 minus epsilon
and getting all the demand.

00:16:14.080 --> 00:16:17.770 align:middle line:84%
But when you have
asymmetric Cournot,

00:16:17.770 --> 00:16:21.470 align:middle line:84%
prices are determined by the
second lowest cost in a market.

00:16:21.470 --> 00:16:23.270 align:middle line:84%
And I think that's the
important intuition.

00:16:23.270 --> 00:16:25.240 align:middle line:84%
That's the second
lowest cost determines

00:16:25.240 --> 00:16:29.630 align:middle line:84%
what happens in markets that
are this very intense price

00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:31.793 align:middle line:84%
competition or
undifferentiated products.

00:16:31.793 --> 00:16:34.460 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Does this change if we
don't have complete information

00:16:34.460 --> 00:16:35.365 align:middle line:90%
on the cost?

00:16:35.365 --> 00:16:38.552 align:middle line:90%


00:16:38.552 --> 00:16:40.010 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON:
Yeah, so let me see.

00:16:40.010 --> 00:16:42.550 align:middle line:84%
So if you did this
model where you had--

00:16:42.550 --> 00:16:44.410 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Like
private info, like you

00:16:44.410 --> 00:16:47.087 align:middle line:84%
don't have any knowledge
on their distribution.

00:16:47.087 --> 00:16:48.670 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: Yeah,
so in some sense,

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:52.360 align:middle line:84%
if you have private
information on costs,

00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:54.910 align:middle line:84%
this is some sense
that's what we normally

00:16:54.910 --> 00:16:57.130 align:middle line:90%
refer to as an auction model.

00:16:57.130 --> 00:16:57.980 align:middle line:90%
It's an auction.

00:16:57.980 --> 00:17:00.230 align:middle line:84%
I don't know what the value
of the painting is to you.

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:02.522 align:middle line:84%
You don't know what the value
of the painting is to me.

00:17:02.522 --> 00:17:05.829 align:middle line:84%
So I bid something,
giving myself

00:17:05.829 --> 00:17:09.640 align:middle line:84%
some expected markup because
if I bid cost, then if I win

00:17:09.640 --> 00:17:12.440 align:middle line:84%
or if I lose, I'm
unhappy both ways.

00:17:12.440 --> 00:17:14.440 align:middle line:84%
And so I bid
something in between.

00:17:14.440 --> 00:17:16.480 align:middle line:84%
In the buying something,
I bid something

00:17:16.480 --> 00:17:19.700 align:middle line:90%
in between my value and 0.

00:17:19.700 --> 00:17:22.480 align:middle line:84%
And you do the same thing,
and we get positive markups

00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:23.710 align:middle line:90%
for that reason.

00:17:23.710 --> 00:17:29.500 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so that
becomes like in the--

00:17:29.500 --> 00:17:32.720 align:middle line:84%
values uniform 0, 1 model, you
bid v over 2 is the equilibrium.

00:17:32.720 --> 00:17:35.450 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense
that is a model.

00:17:35.450 --> 00:17:38.060 align:middle line:84%
You end up reconstructing
something like Cournot

00:17:38.060 --> 00:17:39.460 align:middle line:90%
that you get positive markups.

00:17:39.460 --> 00:17:41.293 align:middle line:84%
And as the number of
firms goes to infinity,

00:17:41.293 --> 00:17:42.150 align:middle line:90%
the markups go to 0.

00:17:42.150 --> 00:17:45.300 align:middle line:84%
So that would be another way to
get a Cournot-like prediction.

00:17:45.300 --> 00:17:47.810 align:middle line:90%


00:17:47.810 --> 00:17:49.670 align:middle line:90%
Anyway, I do talk about--

00:17:49.670 --> 00:17:52.100 align:middle line:84%
I'll do auctions in like
week eight or whatever.

00:17:52.100 --> 00:17:54.268 align:middle line:90%
But we actually get that--

00:17:54.268 --> 00:17:56.060 align:middle line:84%
the place where that
analogy is more direct

00:17:56.060 --> 00:17:57.540 align:middle line:90%
is like procurement auctions.

00:17:57.540 --> 00:18:00.590 align:middle line:84%
You have auctions for
contracts to pave a road

00:18:00.590 --> 00:18:02.180 align:middle line:90%
in the city of Cambridge.

00:18:02.180 --> 00:18:03.970 align:middle line:84%
And those are
allocated by auction.

00:18:03.970 --> 00:18:06.470 align:middle line:84%
And exactly, you don't know
what the rival's costs are going

00:18:06.470 --> 00:18:08.120 align:middle line:90%
to be for that paving job.

00:18:08.120 --> 00:18:11.750 align:middle line:84%
So both of you bid some
markup, hoping to win, yeah.

00:18:11.750 --> 00:18:14.370 align:middle line:90%


00:18:14.370 --> 00:18:21.700 align:middle line:84%
OK, comment on-- again, go
forward another 46 years.

00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:27.180 align:middle line:84%
Comment on-- Cournot comment
on Bertrand competition

00:18:27.180 --> 00:18:28.740 align:middle line:90%
by Harold Hotelling.

00:18:28.740 --> 00:18:32.490 align:middle line:84%
And I wanted to put him here
because Harold Hotelling is

00:18:32.490 --> 00:18:34.950 align:middle line:90%
my great, great grand advisor.

00:18:34.950 --> 00:18:38.240 align:middle line:84%
So Hotelling was
Arrow's advisor.

00:18:38.240 --> 00:18:39.720 align:middle line:90%
Arrow was Maskin's advisor.

00:18:39.720 --> 00:18:42.100 align:middle line:90%
Maskin was Fudenberg's advisor.

00:18:42.100 --> 00:18:43.440 align:middle line:90%
Drew was my advisor.

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:46.860 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, I have some
fondness for Harold Hotelling.

00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:50.340 align:middle line:90%
And he was actually--

00:18:50.340 --> 00:18:54.360 align:middle line:84%
so if you go look at
my advisor family tree,

00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:57.090 align:middle line:84%
they're all mathematicians
beyond some point.

00:18:57.090 --> 00:19:00.385 align:middle line:84%
So you get to all those French
mathematicians, whose names you

00:19:00.385 --> 00:19:02.010 align:middle line:84%
hear in probability
theory or whatever,

00:19:02.010 --> 00:19:06.910 align:middle line:84%
because Hotelling had his PhD in
math rather than in economics.

00:19:06.910 --> 00:19:09.665 align:middle line:84%
So my family tree goes back
three branches in economics.

00:19:09.665 --> 00:19:11.790 align:middle line:84%
And then everyone's a
mathematician from that point

00:19:11.790 --> 00:19:14.740 align:middle line:84%
forward, like for
Hotelling's advisors.

00:19:14.740 --> 00:19:17.503 align:middle line:90%
But anyway, Hotelling, through--

00:19:17.503 --> 00:19:19.420 align:middle line:84%
well, mostly through
having advised Ken Arrow,

00:19:19.420 --> 00:19:23.770 align:middle line:84%
Hotelling does have
over 1,000 descendants,

00:19:23.770 --> 00:19:25.930 align:middle line:90%
grand advisees or whatever.

00:19:25.930 --> 00:19:28.330 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, so Hotelling--
and actually, I

00:19:28.330 --> 00:19:29.870 align:middle line:90%
love Hotelling's writing also.

00:19:29.870 --> 00:19:34.780 align:middle line:84%
You get him to-- he's got
a very, in some sense,

00:19:34.780 --> 00:19:38.050 align:middle line:84%
self-confident but
trying-to-be-understated style

00:19:38.050 --> 00:19:40.400 align:middle line:90%
of this, of--

00:19:40.400 --> 00:19:42.400 align:middle line:84%
"after the work of the
late Professor Edgeworth,

00:19:42.400 --> 00:19:43.858 align:middle line:84%
one may doubt that
anything further

00:19:43.858 --> 00:19:45.760 align:middle line:84%
can be said on the
theory of competition.

00:19:45.760 --> 00:19:48.980 align:middle line:84%
However, one important feature
seems to have escaped scrutiny.

00:19:48.980 --> 00:19:53.068 align:middle line:84%
This is the fact that of all
the purchases of commodities,

00:19:53.068 --> 00:19:54.610 align:middle line:84%
some buy from one,
some from another,

00:19:54.610 --> 00:19:56.560 align:middle line:84%
in spite of the moderate
differences in price."

00:19:56.560 --> 00:19:58.393 align:middle line:84%
And that's saying that
price reaction should

00:19:58.393 --> 00:20:00.850 align:middle line:84%
be continuous rather
than nondifferentiable

00:20:00.850 --> 00:20:03.447 align:middle line:84%
as they are in Bertrand's
model and then working out

00:20:03.447 --> 00:20:04.280 align:middle line:90%
the [? direction. ?]

00:20:04.280 --> 00:20:08.680 align:middle line:84%
So anyway,
Hotelling's model is--

00:20:08.680 --> 00:20:11.980 align:middle line:84%
and in some sense, Hotelling
has opened the path

00:20:11.980 --> 00:20:13.570 align:middle line:84%
that everyone has
followed since,

00:20:13.570 --> 00:20:16.840 align:middle line:84%
which is thinking about
competition in terms

00:20:16.840 --> 00:20:19.840 align:middle line:84%
of heterogeneous
consumer populations

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:25.120 align:middle line:84%
and some underlying type space
of heterogeneous consumers.

00:20:25.120 --> 00:20:28.360 align:middle line:84%
So he had this sort
of line, where he

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:34.750 align:middle line:90%
had consumers arranged on 0, 1.

00:20:34.750 --> 00:20:37.630 align:middle line:84%
And actually, Hotelling spent
a lot of his career at UCLA

00:20:37.630 --> 00:20:40.390 align:middle line:84%
and I think was thinking about
this as a beach in Southern

00:20:40.390 --> 00:20:42.160 align:middle line:90%
California.

00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:44.200 align:middle line:84%
And so Hotelling
did have this model

00:20:44.200 --> 00:20:49.150 align:middle line:84%
you can think of as
you've got the two

00:20:49.150 --> 00:20:53.980 align:middle line:84%
stands on the beach, each one
selling drinks and hot dogs

00:20:53.980 --> 00:20:55.310 align:middle line:90%
and ice cream or whatever.

00:20:55.310 --> 00:20:57.160 align:middle line:84%
And then you have
all the customers,

00:20:57.160 --> 00:20:59.787 align:middle line:84%
who are lying on blankets
somewhere along the beach.

00:20:59.787 --> 00:21:01.870 align:middle line:84%
And then when they want
to get something to drink,

00:21:01.870 --> 00:21:04.020 align:middle line:84%
they pick up, and they
walk to the nearest stand.

00:21:04.020 --> 00:21:05.770 align:middle line:84%
And then they buy
something, and then they

00:21:05.770 --> 00:21:08.170 align:middle line:84%
walk back to where they
were sitting on the beach.

00:21:08.170 --> 00:21:11.470 align:middle line:84%
So his model is we
have these consumers,

00:21:11.470 --> 00:21:14.710 align:middle line:90%
and they have the utilities.

00:21:14.710 --> 00:21:16.130 align:middle line:90%
So I put theta on this axis.

00:21:16.130 --> 00:21:18.750 align:middle line:90%


00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:20.760 align:middle line:84%
So then if I graph
the utility that you

00:21:20.760 --> 00:21:28.080 align:middle line:84%
get buying consumer of type
theta buying from Firm 1,

00:21:28.080 --> 00:21:32.310 align:middle line:84%
I get some utility that
looks like this, v minus t

00:21:32.310 --> 00:21:35.550 align:middle line:90%
times theta minus P1.

00:21:35.550 --> 00:21:40.140 align:middle line:84%
So that would be my utility
of buying from Firm 1.

00:21:40.140 --> 00:21:46.740 align:middle line:84%
And then, consumers also have a
utility of buying from Firm 2.

00:21:46.740 --> 00:21:50.130 align:middle line:90%
So let me on this axis put v--

00:21:50.130 --> 00:21:57.120 align:middle line:84%
this is v minus t1
minus theta minus P2.

00:21:57.120 --> 00:22:03.450 align:middle line:84%
So the argument is t is
my disutility per unit

00:22:03.450 --> 00:22:04.960 align:middle line:90%
of distance walking.

00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:07.410 align:middle line:84%
And so my utility from buying
the product from Firm 1

00:22:07.410 --> 00:22:09.750 align:middle line:84%
is v minus t times
theta, because theta

00:22:09.750 --> 00:22:12.910 align:middle line:90%
captures my distance, minus P1.

00:22:12.910 --> 00:22:16.170 align:middle line:84%
And if I buy from Firm
2, it's v minus t times 1

00:22:16.170 --> 00:22:17.810 align:middle line:90%
minus theta minus P2.

00:22:17.810 --> 00:22:21.200 align:middle line:90%


00:22:21.200 --> 00:22:23.660 align:middle line:84%
And so he did this in
a one-dimensional--

00:22:23.660 --> 00:22:26.660 align:middle line:84%
this one-dimensional type
space captures your preference

00:22:26.660 --> 00:22:28.820 align:middle line:90%
for both goods.

00:22:28.820 --> 00:22:31.580 align:middle line:84%
And so we assume there's a
constant marginal cost c.

00:22:31.580 --> 00:22:35.510 align:middle line:84%
He considered, again,
the Nash equilibrium

00:22:35.510 --> 00:22:38.180 align:middle line:84%
before Nash of firms
simultaneously announced

00:22:38.180 --> 00:22:40.280 align:middle line:90%
prices P1 and P2.

00:22:40.280 --> 00:22:43.410 align:middle line:84%
And then, firms
announce those prices.

00:22:43.410 --> 00:22:44.690 align:middle line:90%
It's a simultaneous move game.

00:22:44.690 --> 00:22:47.107 align:middle line:84%
And then, consumers
decide who to buy from,

00:22:47.107 --> 00:22:48.690 align:middle line:84%
and then the firms
earn their profits.

00:22:48.690 --> 00:22:53.460 align:middle line:84%
And we just look for the
static Nash equilibrium.

00:22:53.460 --> 00:22:59.520 align:middle line:84%
The standard case we discuss
in this model is if v is large,

00:22:59.520 --> 00:23:02.820 align:middle line:84%
then there's some
consumer theta hat

00:23:02.820 --> 00:23:05.430 align:middle line:84%
who's indifferent between
buying the two products.

00:23:05.430 --> 00:23:12.600 align:middle line:84%
So this consumer theta hat has
v minus t theta hat minus P1

00:23:12.600 --> 00:23:19.650 align:middle line:84%
equals v minus t 1 minus
theta hat minus P2.

00:23:19.650 --> 00:23:21.390 align:middle line:84%
So at those prices,
that consumer

00:23:21.390 --> 00:23:23.880 align:middle line:84%
is indifferent between
the two products.

00:23:23.880 --> 00:23:26.610 align:middle line:84%
You solve that equation, and it
tells you that the indifferent

00:23:26.610 --> 00:23:36.550 align:middle line:84%
consumer theta hat is 1/2
plus P2 minus P1 over 2t.

00:23:36.550 --> 00:23:40.332 align:middle line:90%


00:23:40.332 --> 00:23:41.790 align:middle line:84%
With that indifferent
consumer-- do

00:23:41.790 --> 00:23:43.900 align:middle line:84%
I have it on the
next slide or not?

00:23:43.900 --> 00:23:49.067 align:middle line:84%
So with that indifferent
consumer, then you get x1 of P1,

00:23:49.067 --> 00:23:55.780 align:middle line:84%
P2 is 1/2 plus P2
minus P1 over 2t.

00:23:55.780 --> 00:24:07.090 align:middle line:84%
And x2 of P1 P2 is 1/2 minus
P2 minus P1 over 2t, OK?

00:24:07.090 --> 00:24:13.263 align:middle line:84%
The demands add up to 1 if-- as
long as this consumer is willing

00:24:13.263 --> 00:24:14.680 align:middle line:84%
to-- as long as v
is large enough,

00:24:14.680 --> 00:24:16.250 align:middle line:90%
this consumer is willing to buy.

00:24:16.250 --> 00:24:18.560 align:middle line:84%
So everyone buys from
one firm or the other.

00:24:18.560 --> 00:24:21.550 align:middle line:84%
So there's no deadweight
loss in this model.

00:24:21.550 --> 00:24:29.910 align:middle line:84%
Demands end up being independent
of v. So this is if, whatever,

00:24:29.910 --> 00:24:41.700 align:middle line:84%
v minus t times 1/2 plus P2
minus P1 over 2t minus P1

00:24:41.700 --> 00:24:42.820 align:middle line:90%
greater than equal to 0.

00:24:42.820 --> 00:24:47.440 align:middle line:84%
So as long as this indifferent
consumer is willing to buy,

00:24:47.440 --> 00:24:51.490 align:middle line:84%
demands are completely
independent of v. And just

00:24:51.490 --> 00:24:52.960 align:middle line:90%
are these linear--

00:24:52.960 --> 00:24:55.060 align:middle line:90%
these are linear demand curves.

00:24:55.060 --> 00:24:58.090 align:middle line:84%
So what he's done is
he's just proposed

00:24:58.090 --> 00:25:01.210 align:middle line:84%
this model of heterogeneous
consumer populations and said,

00:25:01.210 --> 00:25:04.240 align:middle line:84%
with that heterogeneous
consumer population model,

00:25:04.240 --> 00:25:09.730 align:middle line:84%
we just get price competition
with these particular linear

00:25:09.730 --> 00:25:10.500 align:middle line:90%
demand curves.

00:25:10.500 --> 00:25:14.220 align:middle line:90%


00:25:14.220 --> 00:25:16.720 align:middle line:84%
And what happens when you have
those linear demand curves?

00:25:16.720 --> 00:25:25.290 align:middle line:84%
Well, Firm I is just maximizing
price minus cost times 1/2 plus

00:25:25.290 --> 00:25:27.430 align:middle line:90%
pj minus p over 2t.

00:25:27.430 --> 00:25:30.000 align:middle line:84%
So, if it sets a
higher price, it

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:32.170 align:middle line:84%
gets a higher
markup per consumer.

00:25:32.170 --> 00:25:34.740 align:middle line:84%
But the number of
consumers go down,

00:25:34.740 --> 00:25:38.670 align:middle line:84%
with 1 over 2t being the
slope of the downward-sloping

00:25:38.670 --> 00:25:42.630 align:middle line:84%
individual demand
curve facing it.

00:25:42.630 --> 00:25:45.480 align:middle line:84%
I take the first-order
conditions for this.

00:25:45.480 --> 00:25:46.920 align:middle line:90%
It's just algebra.

00:25:46.920 --> 00:25:49.380 align:middle line:84%
And, given that it's
a linear demand curve,

00:25:49.380 --> 00:25:51.520 align:middle line:84%
we get a quadratic
objective function.

00:25:51.520 --> 00:25:53.850 align:middle line:90%
It has an interior maximum.

00:25:53.850 --> 00:25:57.030 align:middle line:84%
And what we get is the best
response for Firm I to a price

00:25:57.030 --> 00:26:03.460 align:middle line:90%
pj is 1/2 of c plus t plus pj.

00:26:03.460 --> 00:26:12.210 align:middle line:84%
So, if I try to graph what's
happening here, P1, P2,

00:26:12.210 --> 00:26:18.570 align:middle line:84%
so if P1 is 0, the best response
for Firm 2 is 1/2 c plus t is

00:26:18.570 --> 00:26:19.480 align:middle line:90%
positive.

00:26:19.480 --> 00:26:22.240 align:middle line:90%


00:26:22.240 --> 00:26:25.550 align:middle line:84%
And then, it's a line
that's upward sloping.

00:26:25.550 --> 00:26:32.060 align:middle line:84%
So this is the best response
for Firm 2 to a price of P1.

00:26:32.060 --> 00:26:36.100 align:middle line:90%
And this is 1/2 c plus t.

00:26:36.100 --> 00:26:40.470 align:middle line:90%


00:26:40.470 --> 00:26:42.840 align:middle line:84%
And then similarly,
the best response

00:26:42.840 --> 00:26:51.060 align:middle line:84%
for Firm 1 to a price of P2
is going to look like this.

00:26:51.060 --> 00:26:54.630 align:middle line:84%
So I'm graphing Firm
1's best response.

00:26:54.630 --> 00:26:56.520 align:middle line:84%
I'm graphing this
thing backwards,

00:26:56.520 --> 00:26:59.510 align:middle line:84%
as you do in game theory,
where as P2 is higher,

00:26:59.510 --> 00:27:01.230 align:middle line:90%
I'm graphing P1 is higher.

00:27:01.230 --> 00:27:04.570 align:middle line:84%
But this line has slope
1/2 relative to this.

00:27:04.570 --> 00:27:07.740 align:middle line:84%
This one has slope 1/2 if you
view it as a function of this

00:27:07.740 --> 00:27:08.540 align:middle line:90%
line.

00:27:08.540 --> 00:27:11.380 align:middle line:84%
So look at these two
upward-sloping lines.

00:27:11.380 --> 00:27:13.110 align:middle line:90%
They intersect.

00:27:13.110 --> 00:27:14.970 align:middle line:90%
That's the equilibrium.

00:27:14.970 --> 00:27:18.905 align:middle line:84%
And we get P1 star equals
P2 star equals c plus t.

00:27:18.905 --> 00:27:23.540 align:middle line:90%


00:27:23.540 --> 00:27:28.320 align:middle line:84%
So what are the economic
implications here?

00:27:28.320 --> 00:27:32.270 align:middle line:84%
One is that markups are
proportional to the product

00:27:32.270 --> 00:27:34.130 align:middle line:90%
differentiation parameter t.

00:27:34.130 --> 00:27:36.470 align:middle line:84%
So it's the amount of
product differentiation

00:27:36.470 --> 00:27:41.400 align:middle line:84%
in a market that
ends up determining

00:27:41.400 --> 00:27:42.595 align:middle line:90%
how large are the markups?

00:27:42.595 --> 00:27:45.810 align:middle line:90%


00:27:45.810 --> 00:27:48.960 align:middle line:84%
The hotter it is and
the more unpleasant

00:27:48.960 --> 00:27:52.230 align:middle line:84%
it is to walk across the sand,
the higher will be the prices.

00:27:52.230 --> 00:27:56.220 align:middle line:84%
And people today, I think,
use this model not just

00:27:56.220 --> 00:28:00.660 align:middle line:84%
for differentiation in
physical space driving,

00:28:00.660 --> 00:28:04.175 align:middle line:84%
but differentiation in
taste space as well.

00:28:04.175 --> 00:28:05.550 align:middle line:84%
So if you think
about competition

00:28:05.550 --> 00:28:08.800 align:middle line:84%
between Coke and Pepsi,
how much do you like Coke?

00:28:08.800 --> 00:28:09.970 align:middle line:90%
How much do you like Pepsi?

00:28:09.970 --> 00:28:14.342 align:middle line:84%
There are some people who
love Coke and dislike Pepsi.

00:28:14.342 --> 00:28:16.300 align:middle line:84%
There are some who love
Pepsi and dislike Coke.

00:28:16.300 --> 00:28:17.675 align:middle line:84%
There are some
who are completely

00:28:17.675 --> 00:28:18.850 align:middle line:90%
indifferent between the two.

00:28:18.850 --> 00:28:22.020 align:middle line:84%
The amount of
taste heterogeneity

00:28:22.020 --> 00:28:24.510 align:middle line:90%
is what determines the markups.

00:28:24.510 --> 00:28:26.580 align:middle line:84%
And numerically, with
these linear demand curves,

00:28:26.580 --> 00:28:28.860 align:middle line:84%
it's the amount by
which people at one end

00:28:28.860 --> 00:28:30.247 align:middle line:90%
of the line versus the other--

00:28:30.247 --> 00:28:31.830 align:middle line:84%
it's the relative
preference of people

00:28:31.830 --> 00:28:33.670 align:middle line:84%
at one end of the
line versus the other.

00:28:33.670 --> 00:28:37.060 align:middle line:84%
This person thinks Pepsi
is t better than Coke.

00:28:37.060 --> 00:28:39.517 align:middle line:84%
That person thinks Coke is
t better than Pepsi. t is

00:28:39.517 --> 00:28:40.475 align:middle line:90%
the equilibrium markup.

00:28:40.475 --> 00:28:43.746 align:middle line:90%


00:28:43.746 --> 00:28:47.780 align:middle line:84%
You can do an n firm
circular version of this.

00:28:47.780 --> 00:28:49.850 align:middle line:90%
So imagine that you have--

00:28:49.850 --> 00:28:53.560 align:middle line:84%
obviously, there are lots of
parts of the United States--

00:28:53.560 --> 00:28:55.880 align:middle line:84%
well, obviously, you'd
have to go all the way down

00:28:55.880 --> 00:28:56.810 align:middle line:90%
to South America.

00:28:56.810 --> 00:28:58.893 align:middle line:84%
And some parts, no one's
going to be on the beach.

00:28:58.893 --> 00:29:01.460 align:middle line:84%
But you could imagine
a giant beach that's--

00:29:01.460 --> 00:29:03.890 align:middle line:84%
we have a resort island
where the beach is just

00:29:03.890 --> 00:29:06.800 align:middle line:90%
circle around the whole thing.

00:29:06.800 --> 00:29:11.240 align:middle line:84%
If you put n firms
around this, it's

00:29:11.240 --> 00:29:14.510 align:middle line:84%
as if the competition between
any of these two firms

00:29:14.510 --> 00:29:17.660 align:middle line:84%
is now going to be
like t times 1 over n

00:29:17.660 --> 00:29:20.660 align:middle line:84%
is going to be the effective
distance between them.

00:29:20.660 --> 00:29:23.450 align:middle line:84%
And so if t times 1 over n
becomes the effective distance

00:29:23.450 --> 00:29:28.940 align:middle line:84%
between them, you're
going to get P star equals

00:29:28.940 --> 00:29:31.730 align:middle line:90%
c plus t times 1 over n.

00:29:31.730 --> 00:29:35.492 align:middle line:84%
So this is a model where
it can be like Cournot.

00:29:35.492 --> 00:29:36.950 align:middle line:84%
And, as you put
more and more firms

00:29:36.950 --> 00:29:39.620 align:middle line:84%
into the same finite
dimensional product space,

00:29:39.620 --> 00:29:41.750 align:middle line:84%
markups decline
and are continuous

00:29:41.750 --> 00:29:44.470 align:middle line:84%
with perfect competition
as n goes to infinity.

00:29:44.470 --> 00:29:47.895 align:middle line:90%


00:29:47.895 --> 00:29:49.270 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, I'll just
say this later.

00:29:49.270 --> 00:29:50.187 align:middle line:90%
Actions are strategic.

00:29:50.187 --> 00:29:53.462 align:middle line:84%
Complements just means
that the reaction curves

00:29:53.462 --> 00:29:55.420 align:middle line:84%
look different from how
they looked in Cournot.

00:29:55.420 --> 00:29:57.613 align:middle line:84%
Here, it's an intersection
of upward-sloping lines.

00:29:57.613 --> 00:29:59.863 align:middle line:84%
Cournot was an intersection
of downward-sloping lines.

00:29:59.863 --> 00:30:02.640 align:middle line:90%


00:30:02.640 --> 00:30:05.850 align:middle line:84%
Understanding some of the
implications of the model,

00:30:05.850 --> 00:30:10.080 align:middle line:84%
that's going to
matter later, OK?

00:30:10.080 --> 00:30:15.380 align:middle line:84%
OK, any questions on
Hotelling's model?

00:30:15.380 --> 00:30:19.160 align:middle line:90%
All right, OK.

00:30:19.160 --> 00:30:24.260 align:middle line:84%
OK, so vertical differentiation,
final thing we think of

00:30:24.260 --> 00:30:28.700 align:middle line:84%
is that there's many
examples like Coke and Pepsi.

00:30:28.700 --> 00:30:30.920 align:middle line:84%
Clearly, one is the better
product, and one is--

00:30:30.920 --> 00:30:33.470 align:middle line:84%
sorry, two products are
just a matter of taste,

00:30:33.470 --> 00:30:35.180 align:middle line:90%
whether you a versus b.

00:30:35.180 --> 00:30:36.710 align:middle line:84%
There are other
examples we think

00:30:36.710 --> 00:30:39.050 align:middle line:84%
of where differentiation
consists of one product

00:30:39.050 --> 00:30:41.630 align:middle line:90%
is better and one is worse.

00:30:41.630 --> 00:30:49.940 align:middle line:84%
So if you think about do
people want to buy the BMW,

00:30:49.940 --> 00:30:53.120 align:middle line:84%
or do they want the
Toyota Yaris or something?

00:30:53.120 --> 00:30:55.880 align:middle line:84%
Everybody would
rather have the BMW.

00:30:55.880 --> 00:30:57.740 align:middle line:84%
Everyone admits
that this product a

00:30:57.740 --> 00:30:59.258 align:middle line:90%
is better than product b.

00:30:59.258 --> 00:31:00.800 align:middle line:84%
But the question
is, how much are you

00:31:00.800 --> 00:31:03.530 align:middle line:84%
willing to pay for product
a versus product b?

00:31:03.530 --> 00:31:06.352 align:middle line:84%
You could have, again,
maybe some people

00:31:06.352 --> 00:31:07.310 align:middle line:90%
still like flip phones.

00:31:07.310 --> 00:31:09.830 align:middle line:84%
But you have the
modern phone competing

00:31:09.830 --> 00:31:12.910 align:middle line:84%
against the old flip
phone, with just a keypad

00:31:12.910 --> 00:31:14.160 align:middle line:90%
and a little screen like this.

00:31:14.160 --> 00:31:17.730 align:middle line:84%
Everyone agrees that the modern
phone is the better phone.

00:31:17.730 --> 00:31:19.260 align:middle line:84%
But people just
differ in how much

00:31:19.260 --> 00:31:21.600 align:middle line:84%
they're willing to pay
for the better product.

00:31:21.600 --> 00:31:24.813 align:middle line:84%
So the consumer
preferences here are going

00:31:24.813 --> 00:31:25.980 align:middle line:90%
to be vertical rather than--

00:31:25.980 --> 00:31:27.855 align:middle line:84%
what we call vertical
rather than horizontal.

00:31:27.855 --> 00:31:29.740 align:middle line:90%
So do this example.

00:31:29.740 --> 00:31:31.890 align:middle line:90%
I've got two firms.

00:31:31.890 --> 00:31:33.420 align:middle line:90%
They have goods of quality.

00:31:33.420 --> 00:31:35.290 align:middle line:90%
SL is my flip phone.

00:31:35.290 --> 00:31:37.290 align:middle line:90%
SH is my iPhone.

00:31:37.290 --> 00:31:39.683 align:middle line:84%
And the low-quality phone,
everyone would agree,

00:31:39.683 --> 00:31:41.225 align:middle line:84%
is worse than the
high-quality phone.

00:31:41.225 --> 00:31:43.790 align:middle line:90%


00:31:43.790 --> 00:31:46.130 align:middle line:84%
We have consumers
have types, again,

00:31:46.130 --> 00:31:49.230 align:middle line:84%
distributed on a unit interval,
or sorry, nonunit interval

00:31:49.230 --> 00:31:51.800 align:middle line:84%
now, theta lower
bar, theta upper bar.

00:31:51.800 --> 00:31:53.480 align:middle line:84%
And the utility
functions are going

00:31:53.480 --> 00:31:56.900 align:middle line:84%
to be the consumer-- utility of
a consumer of type theta buying

00:31:56.900 --> 00:32:00.050 align:middle line:90%
product i is theta si minus pi.

00:32:00.050 --> 00:32:04.247 align:middle line:84%
So the theta is your marginal
value of a unit of quality.

00:32:04.247 --> 00:32:06.080 align:middle line:84%
You could also just
think of this as a model

00:32:06.080 --> 00:32:08.300 align:middle line:84%
where consumers are
differentiated by income

00:32:08.300 --> 00:32:13.070 align:middle line:84%
and have a different
marginal value of income.

00:32:13.070 --> 00:32:15.860 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, they have
all the same utility

00:32:15.860 --> 00:32:17.370 align:middle line:84%
that they get from
higher quality.

00:32:17.370 --> 00:32:19.412 align:middle line:84%
But just some people have
more money than others.

00:32:19.412 --> 00:32:21.980 align:middle line:84%
They have a greater disutility
of spending money, OK?

00:32:21.980 --> 00:32:24.960 align:middle line:90%


00:32:24.960 --> 00:32:27.010 align:middle line:84%
OK, and so consumers
have this utility

00:32:27.010 --> 00:32:29.790 align:middle line:84%
if they buy from Firm I.
They get 0 utility if they

00:32:29.790 --> 00:32:33.570 align:middle line:90%
buy from neither firm at all.

00:32:33.570 --> 00:32:35.850 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to, for simplicity,
assume a unit mass

00:32:35.850 --> 00:32:40.120 align:middle line:84%
of consumers, a unit density of
consumers and an interval theta

00:32:40.120 --> 00:32:41.315 align:middle line:90%
lower bar, theta upper bar.

00:32:41.315 --> 00:32:43.950 align:middle line:90%


00:32:43.950 --> 00:32:47.640 align:middle line:84%
OK, so what's going
on here is, here, I'm

00:32:47.640 --> 00:32:50.610 align:middle line:84%
typically going to
have my theta space--

00:32:50.610 --> 00:32:55.510 align:middle line:84%
often, we have our
theta space start at 0.

00:32:55.510 --> 00:32:58.505 align:middle line:84%
But here, everybody in my
model is going to like quality.

00:32:58.505 --> 00:32:59.880 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to
think of a model.

00:32:59.880 --> 00:33:01.588 align:middle line:84%
We have this sort of
interval theta lower

00:33:01.588 --> 00:33:05.010 align:middle line:90%
bar, theta upper bar.

00:33:05.010 --> 00:33:10.080 align:middle line:84%
And so consumers are
arrayed on this interval

00:33:10.080 --> 00:33:11.165 align:middle line:90%
of positive values.

00:33:11.165 --> 00:33:13.710 align:middle line:90%


00:33:13.710 --> 00:33:19.770 align:middle line:84%
So again, consider the same
game that Bertrand and Hotelling

00:33:19.770 --> 00:33:20.610 align:middle line:90%
considered.

00:33:20.610 --> 00:33:24.060 align:middle line:84%
Firms simultaneously
choose prices PL and PH.

00:33:24.060 --> 00:33:26.130 align:middle line:84%
Taking the other
firm's price is fixed,

00:33:26.130 --> 00:33:28.470 align:middle line:84%
consumers decide
who to buy from.

00:33:28.470 --> 00:33:30.850 align:middle line:90%
And then firms earn profits.

00:33:30.850 --> 00:33:33.310 align:middle line:90%
That's the end of the game.

00:33:33.310 --> 00:33:36.340 align:middle line:90%
What do the utilities look like?

00:33:36.340 --> 00:33:42.820 align:middle line:84%
Here, let me first
draw a line like this.

00:33:42.820 --> 00:33:47.080 align:middle line:84%
This is the line
theta SL minus PL.

00:33:47.080 --> 00:33:52.000 align:middle line:84%
So it starts at
negative Pl, and then it

00:33:52.000 --> 00:33:54.830 align:middle line:90%
goes up with a slope of SL.

00:33:54.830 --> 00:33:56.700 align:middle line:84%
This is the utility
that you get--

00:33:56.700 --> 00:33:59.660 align:middle line:90%


00:33:59.660 --> 00:34:01.860 align:middle line:90%
this is UL of theta.

00:34:01.860 --> 00:34:10.698 align:middle line:84%
That's the utility you get
buying from Firm L. Equalas

00:34:10.698 --> 00:34:13.120 align:middle line:90%
UL of theta.

00:34:13.120 --> 00:34:15.670 align:middle line:84%
OK, then other line
I'm going to draw

00:34:15.670 --> 00:34:20.350 align:middle line:84%
is Firm H has the
higher quality product.

00:34:20.350 --> 00:34:23.710 align:middle line:84%
So if you had type
0, which nobody does,

00:34:23.710 --> 00:34:27.370 align:middle line:84%
you'd get utility negative
PH from buying that product.

00:34:27.370 --> 00:34:30.670 align:middle line:84%
But then I'm going to assume
that's a steeper-sloping line,

00:34:30.670 --> 00:34:34.750 align:middle line:90%
like this, theta SH minus PH.

00:34:34.750 --> 00:34:39.790 align:middle line:84%
It's a steeper-sloping line
because SH is bigger than SL,

00:34:39.790 --> 00:34:43.690 align:middle line:90%
and the slope is S, OK?

00:34:43.690 --> 00:34:45.219 align:middle line:90%
So what happens in this model?

00:34:45.219 --> 00:34:48.050 align:middle line:84%
Again, there's some--
in equilibrium,

00:34:48.050 --> 00:34:52.730 align:middle line:84%
there's be some indifferent
consumer of type theta hat.

00:34:52.730 --> 00:34:58.010 align:middle line:84%
Consumers with types above
theta hat, so these guys

00:34:58.010 --> 00:35:03.080 align:middle line:90%
all buy the high-quality good.

00:35:03.080 --> 00:35:05.030 align:middle line:84%
These consumers,
the upper envelope,

00:35:05.030 --> 00:35:10.720 align:middle line:84%
is these guys buy
the low-quality good.

00:35:10.720 --> 00:35:13.330 align:middle line:84%
As I've drawn it, there's
another point that's

00:35:13.330 --> 00:35:15.070 align:middle line:90%
of importance called theta--

00:35:15.070 --> 00:35:15.940 align:middle line:90%
I'll call it theta--

00:35:15.940 --> 00:35:19.320 align:middle line:84%
I named theta prime
in the slides.

00:35:19.320 --> 00:35:22.380 align:middle line:84%
Anyone with a type
below theta prime

00:35:22.380 --> 00:35:26.280 align:middle line:84%
is going to buy nothing
because neither good is worth

00:35:26.280 --> 00:35:27.605 align:middle line:90%
paying what's being charged.

00:35:27.605 --> 00:35:31.090 align:middle line:90%


00:35:31.090 --> 00:35:34.780 align:middle line:84%
I've drawn this in the case
where theta prime is below theta

00:35:34.780 --> 00:35:38.320 align:middle line:84%
lower bar, and theta hat
is between theta lower

00:35:38.320 --> 00:35:40.270 align:middle line:90%
bar and theta upper bar.

00:35:40.270 --> 00:35:42.970 align:middle line:84%
And in that case,
what are the demands?

00:35:42.970 --> 00:35:44.950 align:middle line:84%
The demand for the
high-quality product

00:35:44.950 --> 00:35:48.170 align:middle line:84%
is just theta upper
bar minus theta hat.

00:35:48.170 --> 00:35:50.150 align:middle line:84%
And the demand for the
low-quality product

00:35:50.150 --> 00:35:53.520 align:middle line:84%
is theta hat minus
theta lower bar.

00:35:53.520 --> 00:35:55.890 align:middle line:84%
If theta prime were
to the right of this,

00:35:55.890 --> 00:35:57.510 align:middle line:84%
the demand for the
low-quality product

00:35:57.510 --> 00:35:59.600 align:middle line:84%
would be theta hat
minus theta prime.

00:35:59.600 --> 00:36:02.710 align:middle line:90%


00:36:02.710 --> 00:36:07.510 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, I drew it like this,
so this becomes the equations.

00:36:07.510 --> 00:36:16.780 align:middle line:84%
And then again, this theta hat
satisfies theta hat SH minus PH

00:36:16.780 --> 00:36:20.830 align:middle line:90%
equals theta hat SL minus PL.

00:36:20.830 --> 00:36:28.420 align:middle line:84%
So that tells you that
theta hat times SH minus SL

00:36:28.420 --> 00:36:32.200 align:middle line:90%
equals PH minus PL.

00:36:32.200 --> 00:36:37.240 align:middle line:84%
That gives you this
expression here for what

00:36:37.240 --> 00:36:39.970 align:middle line:90%
the demand is for product H.

00:36:39.970 --> 00:36:45.430 align:middle line:84%
So again, it's a
story, and it's a story

00:36:45.430 --> 00:36:48.160 align:middle line:84%
that just basically what is
done is it's just given us,

00:36:48.160 --> 00:36:50.830 align:middle line:90%
again, two linear demand curves.

00:36:50.830 --> 00:36:53.740 align:middle line:84%
These are both-- they've got
other parameters in them,

00:36:53.740 --> 00:36:54.760 align:middle line:90%
and it's not so obvious.

00:36:54.760 --> 00:36:55.570 align:middle line:90%
But you just look at it.

00:36:55.570 --> 00:36:57.070 align:middle line:84%
It's like, yeah,
that's just-- it's

00:36:57.070 --> 00:36:59.050 align:middle line:90%
a linear function of PL and PH.

00:36:59.050 --> 00:37:00.550 align:middle line:84%
It's a linear
function of PL and PH.

00:37:00.550 --> 00:37:03.420 align:middle line:90%


00:37:03.420 --> 00:37:06.360 align:middle line:84%
Hotelling was a
symmetric linear model.

00:37:06.360 --> 00:37:08.990 align:middle line:84%
The two demand curves
looked exactly the same.

00:37:08.990 --> 00:37:11.850 align:middle line:84%
Here, the demand
curves look different.

00:37:11.850 --> 00:37:17.598 align:middle line:84%
In particular, if the
prices were equal,

00:37:17.598 --> 00:37:19.640 align:middle line:84%
everyone would buy from
Firm H. So in some sense,

00:37:19.640 --> 00:37:21.680 align:middle line:84%
Firm H's demand is
higher than Firm L's

00:37:21.680 --> 00:37:23.600 align:middle line:90%
holding the price is fixed.

00:37:23.600 --> 00:37:25.495 align:middle line:84%
But that's what the
equations look like.

00:37:25.495 --> 00:37:32.030 align:middle line:90%


00:37:32.030 --> 00:37:35.640 align:middle line:84%
But it's just a shift
of demand curves.

00:37:35.640 --> 00:37:38.120 align:middle line:84%
One demand curve is higher,
but the demand curves

00:37:38.120 --> 00:37:40.680 align:middle line:84%
are both linear with the same
slope and the two variables.

00:37:40.680 --> 00:37:44.920 align:middle line:90%


00:37:44.920 --> 00:37:46.920 align:middle line:84%
Any question on that,
getting from the equations

00:37:46.920 --> 00:37:49.560 align:middle line:90%
to the slopes?

00:37:49.560 --> 00:37:51.930 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, that's what our
demand model looks like.

00:37:51.930 --> 00:37:53.680 align:middle line:84%
And then again, the
question is like, what

00:37:53.680 --> 00:37:55.810 align:middle line:90%
happens in this competition?

00:37:55.810 --> 00:38:02.452 align:middle line:84%
And again, it's just
it's solving a--

00:38:02.452 --> 00:38:05.230 align:middle line:84%
it's just solving two
equations and two unknowns

00:38:05.230 --> 00:38:07.610 align:middle line:84%
to find what the
demand curves are.

00:38:07.610 --> 00:38:14.020 align:middle line:84%
It really is not so different
here from what Hotelling did.

00:38:14.020 --> 00:38:28.870 align:middle line:84%
So if I plot P1 and P2 again,
so the best response to Firm H

00:38:28.870 --> 00:38:31.090 align:middle line:84%
of a price PL turns out
to be-- if you remember,

00:38:31.090 --> 00:38:34.270 align:middle line:84%
the other one was
1/2 p plus c plus t.

00:38:34.270 --> 00:38:39.640 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out to be 1/2 p
plus c plus, instead of t,

00:38:39.640 --> 00:38:43.480 align:middle line:84%
we get theta upper
bar minus SH minus SL,

00:38:43.480 --> 00:38:46.450 align:middle line:84%
which is like the amount of
differentiation that there is

00:38:46.450 --> 00:38:49.660 align:middle line:84%
between the two products from
the perspective of the theta

00:38:49.660 --> 00:38:50.990 align:middle line:90%
upper bar consumer.

00:38:50.990 --> 00:38:53.650 align:middle line:84%
So it's a lot like
p plus c plus t

00:38:53.650 --> 00:38:55.263 align:middle line:84%
but just with the
amount of difference

00:38:55.263 --> 00:38:57.430 align:middle line:84%
between the products here
being from the perspective

00:38:57.430 --> 00:39:00.640 align:middle line:90%
of the high theta consumer.

00:39:00.640 --> 00:39:06.670 align:middle line:84%
And so what that looks like
is we start again here.

00:39:06.670 --> 00:39:11.200 align:middle line:90%
So we can put PL here, PH here.

00:39:11.200 --> 00:39:16.420 align:middle line:84%
So it starts at 1/2 PL plus
c plus something or other.

00:39:16.420 --> 00:39:19.030 align:middle line:84%
So it starts at
some positive thing,

00:39:19.030 --> 00:39:21.520 align:middle line:84%
and it goes up with
a slope of 1/2.

00:39:21.520 --> 00:39:27.420 align:middle line:84%
So this is the best response
for Firm H to a price of PL.

00:39:27.420 --> 00:39:37.670 align:middle line:84%
And this is 1/2 of c plus theta
upper bar times SH minus SL.

00:39:37.670 --> 00:39:44.640 align:middle line:84%
Now, Firm L's best response is
1/2 of p plus c minus something.

00:39:44.640 --> 00:39:46.640 align:middle line:84%
And so the minus
something is again

00:39:46.640 --> 00:39:49.550 align:middle line:84%
related to how much
differentiation there is.

00:39:49.550 --> 00:39:53.530 align:middle line:84%
Notice because when PH is 0,
this becomes 1/2 of c minus

00:39:53.530 --> 00:39:54.030 align:middle line:90%
something.

00:39:54.030 --> 00:39:55.880 align:middle line:84%
This thing could
even be negative,

00:39:55.880 --> 00:39:58.310 align:middle line:90%
or it's certainly below c.

00:39:58.310 --> 00:40:02.930 align:middle line:84%
And it could even be a negative
number if differentiate,

00:40:02.930 --> 00:40:05.973 align:middle line:84%
depending on how big
differentiation is.

00:40:05.973 --> 00:40:08.390 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, I'll draw it as
starting as a positive number,

00:40:08.390 --> 00:40:10.490 align:middle line:84%
but it starts from
some smaller amount.

00:40:10.490 --> 00:40:14.490 align:middle line:84%
And then it also goes
up at a slope of 1/2.

00:40:14.490 --> 00:40:20.160 align:middle line:90%
So we get BR H--

00:40:20.160 --> 00:40:21.790 align:middle line:90%
BRL of PH.

00:40:21.790 --> 00:40:24.870 align:middle line:90%
Looks like that.

00:40:24.870 --> 00:40:29.070 align:middle line:84%
And it starts from 1/2 of c
minus theta lower bar times SH

00:40:29.070 --> 00:40:31.700 align:middle line:90%
minus SL.

00:40:31.700 --> 00:40:35.630 align:middle line:84%
And, given these things both
have slope of 1/2, again,

00:40:35.630 --> 00:40:37.250 align:middle line:90%
there's a unique intersection.

00:40:37.250 --> 00:40:38.820 align:middle line:90%
And it's right there.

00:40:38.820 --> 00:40:43.820 align:middle line:84%
And you can think of this
as PL star comma PH star.

00:40:43.820 --> 00:40:48.200 align:middle line:84%
And what we get is this
asymmetric equilibrium.

00:40:48.200 --> 00:40:50.720 align:middle line:84%
The formulas look the
same, but this one's

00:40:50.720 --> 00:40:54.050 align:middle line:84%
got twice the bigger number
minus the smaller number.

00:40:54.050 --> 00:40:55.940 align:middle line:84%
And this one has 1
times the bigger number

00:40:55.940 --> 00:40:57.570 align:middle line:90%
minus twice the smaller number.

00:40:57.570 --> 00:41:01.310 align:middle line:84%
So this is a bigger
number than this one.

00:41:01.310 --> 00:41:06.620 align:middle line:84%
And so what we get is, again,
vertical differentiation, just

00:41:06.620 --> 00:41:09.110 align:middle line:84%
like horizontal
differentiation, it

00:41:09.110 --> 00:41:12.930 align:middle line:84%
creates finite
demand elasticities.

00:41:12.930 --> 00:41:14.470 align:middle line:90%
It creates positive markups.

00:41:14.470 --> 00:41:17.270 align:middle line:84%
If you put uniform distributions
on the vertical tastes,

00:41:17.270 --> 00:41:19.040 align:middle line:84%
it can be linear
demand curves, just

00:41:19.040 --> 00:41:22.460 align:middle line:90%
like the horizontal model is.

00:41:22.460 --> 00:41:26.450 align:middle line:84%
In equilibrium, the
differentiation, again,

00:41:26.450 --> 00:41:28.660 align:middle line:90%
leads to positive markups.

00:41:28.660 --> 00:41:31.490 align:middle line:84%
Leads to positive markups, but
it leads to positive markups

00:41:31.490 --> 00:41:34.100 align:middle line:90%
where Firm H is favored.

00:41:34.100 --> 00:41:35.436 align:middle line:90%
Firm L is disfavored.

00:41:35.436 --> 00:41:38.230 align:middle line:90%


00:41:38.230 --> 00:41:42.340 align:middle line:84%
Notice also that if theta
upper bar and theta lower bar

00:41:42.340 --> 00:41:45.250 align:middle line:84%
are too close together,
this doesn't work,

00:41:45.250 --> 00:41:47.710 align:middle line:84%
and Firm L just has to
drop out of the market

00:41:47.710 --> 00:41:51.760 align:middle line:84%
because the equilibrium looks
like theta upper bar minus 2

00:41:51.760 --> 00:41:53.830 align:middle line:90%
theta lower bar over 3.

00:41:53.830 --> 00:41:57.220 align:middle line:84%
As theta lower bar approaches
1/2 of theta upper bar,

00:41:57.220 --> 00:41:59.800 align:middle line:90%
Firm 2 is just pricing at cost.

00:41:59.800 --> 00:42:07.000 align:middle line:84%
If firm theta lower bar was even
closer to theta upper bar, what

00:42:07.000 --> 00:42:09.620 align:middle line:84%
you'd get in equilibrium is
Firm L would just be shut out.

00:42:09.620 --> 00:42:11.867 align:middle line:84%
It would be driven
down to pricing--

00:42:11.867 --> 00:42:14.450 align:middle line:84%
to play [INAUDIBLE], that would
have to be pricing below cost.

00:42:14.450 --> 00:42:16.075 align:middle line:84%
It eventually doesn't
price below cost.

00:42:16.075 --> 00:42:18.750 align:middle line:84%
It can't compete
in the market, OK?

00:42:18.750 --> 00:42:23.100 align:middle line:84%
So vertical differentiation,
like horizontal differentiation,

00:42:23.100 --> 00:42:25.660 align:middle line:84%
produces finite
demand elasticities.

00:42:25.660 --> 00:42:34.530 align:middle line:84%
It can give us, again,
standard Cournot-like markups.

00:42:34.530 --> 00:42:39.130 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't give us a
model where Cournot is--

00:42:39.130 --> 00:42:42.170 align:middle line:84%
it's like Cournot had that
continuity n goes to infinity.

00:42:42.170 --> 00:42:43.820 align:middle line:90%
Price heads to cost.

00:42:43.820 --> 00:42:46.180 align:middle line:84%
Here, this doesn't happen
because there's only room

00:42:46.180 --> 00:42:49.000 align:middle line:90%
for a finite number of firms.

00:42:49.000 --> 00:42:50.560 align:middle line:84%
You start taking
this space, and you

00:42:50.560 --> 00:42:53.950 align:middle line:84%
try to have three or four or
five or 10 different firms

00:42:53.950 --> 00:42:54.800 align:middle line:90%
selling to this.

00:42:54.800 --> 00:42:57.740 align:middle line:84%
At some point, the firms
get too close together.

00:42:57.740 --> 00:43:00.460 align:middle line:84%
The firms that are below just
get shut out of the market

00:43:00.460 --> 00:43:01.340 align:middle line:90%
and can't compete.

00:43:01.340 --> 00:43:02.750 align:middle line:84%
And so it's only the
high-quality firms

00:43:02.750 --> 00:43:03.833 align:middle line:90%
that are making any sales.

00:43:03.833 --> 00:43:06.880 align:middle line:84%
And so there's some
minimum level of markups

00:43:06.880 --> 00:43:08.560 align:middle line:84%
you're going to
have to get there.

00:43:08.560 --> 00:43:11.800 align:middle line:84%
Although, obviously,
as the firms

00:43:11.800 --> 00:43:14.350 align:middle line:84%
crowd the space, the
difference between the SH firm

00:43:14.350 --> 00:43:16.142 align:middle line:84%
and the SH minus epsilon
firm, you're only

00:43:16.142 --> 00:43:17.350 align:middle line:90%
going to get epsilon profits.

00:43:17.350 --> 00:43:23.970 align:middle line:84%
But you're going to get it with
just a few firms active, OK?

00:43:23.970 --> 00:43:27.510 align:middle line:84%
OK, and obviously, I think
modern IO-- in reality,

00:43:27.510 --> 00:43:29.940 align:middle line:84%
we think that vertical
differentiation

00:43:29.940 --> 00:43:33.370 align:middle line:84%
and horizontal differentiation
are present in most markets.

00:43:33.370 --> 00:43:37.097 align:middle line:84%
We think most products,
there's some extent

00:43:37.097 --> 00:43:39.180 align:middle line:84%
to which we think product
a is better than product

00:43:39.180 --> 00:43:41.520 align:middle line:84%
b that everyone would
agree on or almost everyone

00:43:41.520 --> 00:43:42.160 align:middle line:90%
would agree on.

00:43:42.160 --> 00:43:44.463 align:middle line:84%
And then there's other
horizontal aspects.

00:43:44.463 --> 00:43:47.130 align:middle line:84%
Everyone wants a phone that has
better battery life and a better

00:43:47.130 --> 00:43:49.135 align:middle line:84%
screen resolution
and a better camera.

00:43:49.135 --> 00:43:50.760 align:middle line:84%
But then there are
other things, people

00:43:50.760 --> 00:43:53.730 align:middle line:84%
like the look and feel of a
versus b and are going to have--

00:43:53.730 --> 00:43:55.832 align:middle line:84%
know the operating
system of a versus b

00:43:55.832 --> 00:43:58.040 align:middle line:84%
and are going to have
horizontal and vertical tastes.

00:43:58.040 --> 00:44:04.510 align:middle line:90%


00:44:04.510 --> 00:44:08.210 align:middle line:84%
So let me go back to
horizontal differentiation now.

00:44:08.210 --> 00:44:13.330 align:middle line:84%
And this is motivated by how the
empirical literature and IO has

00:44:13.330 --> 00:44:14.180 align:middle line:90%
developed.

00:44:14.180 --> 00:44:19.920 align:middle line:90%
So generally, the standard--

00:44:19.920 --> 00:44:25.990 align:middle line:84%
Hotelling is a very nice model
of competition on a line.

00:44:25.990 --> 00:44:29.040 align:middle line:84%
Hotelling is not as good a
model once you have three firms

00:44:29.040 --> 00:44:30.335 align:middle line:90%
instead of two firms.

00:44:30.335 --> 00:44:31.710 align:middle line:84%
Because you have
three firms, you

00:44:31.710 --> 00:44:35.100 align:middle line:84%
have to think about where do we
put the three firms and product

00:44:35.100 --> 00:44:35.640 align:middle line:90%
space.

00:44:35.640 --> 00:44:38.250 align:middle line:90%
One thing you could do is--

00:44:38.250 --> 00:44:39.900 align:middle line:90%
what did I do with my eraser?

00:44:39.900 --> 00:44:42.090 align:middle line:90%
There it is.

00:44:42.090 --> 00:44:46.580 align:middle line:84%
You could, if you're someone
who likes high school geometry,

00:44:46.580 --> 00:44:49.565 align:middle line:84%
you could go back to your
youth and say, OK, here's

00:44:49.565 --> 00:44:50.440 align:middle line:90%
what I'm going to do.

00:44:50.440 --> 00:44:53.220 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to have the
three-firm Hotelling model.

00:44:53.220 --> 00:44:58.560 align:middle line:84%
And I have Firm 1 here,
Firm 2 here, Firm 3 here.

00:44:58.560 --> 00:45:01.290 align:middle line:84%
And there's going to be
this set of consumers

00:45:01.290 --> 00:45:03.540 align:middle line:84%
who are indifferent
between Firm 1 and Firm 2

00:45:03.540 --> 00:45:06.300 align:middle line:84%
based on their prices
and a set of firms

00:45:06.300 --> 00:45:11.070 align:middle line:84%
indifferent between Firm 1
and 3 based on their prices

00:45:11.070 --> 00:45:16.030 align:middle line:84%
and a set of firms indifferent
between Firm 1 and 2

00:45:16.030 --> 00:45:19.150 align:middle line:90%
based on their prices.

00:45:19.150 --> 00:45:22.000 align:middle line:84%
I think that has to go
through the same point.

00:45:22.000 --> 00:45:24.603 align:middle line:84%
And so we're going to get
these three different regions.

00:45:24.603 --> 00:45:27.110 align:middle line:90%


00:45:27.110 --> 00:45:31.940 align:middle line:84%
So in equilibrium,
these guys buy from 1.

00:45:31.940 --> 00:45:34.730 align:middle line:90%
These guys buy from 2.

00:45:34.730 --> 00:45:37.268 align:middle line:90%
And these guys buy from Firm 3.

00:45:37.268 --> 00:45:39.560 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to use everything
I remember from high school

00:45:39.560 --> 00:45:41.390 align:middle line:84%
geometry to figure out
the areas of those things

00:45:41.390 --> 00:45:42.765 align:middle line:84%
and how the areas
of those things

00:45:42.765 --> 00:45:45.150 align:middle line:84%
are functions of
P1 and P2 and P3.

00:45:45.150 --> 00:45:48.170 align:middle line:84%
And then, when I get to 4, I'm
going to make it a tetrahedron.

00:45:48.170 --> 00:45:50.520 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to draw all
the volumes of those things.

00:45:50.520 --> 00:45:54.350 align:middle line:84%
But it quickly becomes
just an intractable mess

00:45:54.350 --> 00:45:55.590 align:middle line:90%
with lots of corners.

00:45:55.590 --> 00:45:58.680 align:middle line:84%
And you also think, why were we
doing this in the first place?

00:45:58.680 --> 00:46:01.010 align:middle line:84%
It wasn't like we felt that
people were uniformly--

00:46:01.010 --> 00:46:03.390 align:middle line:84%
uniformly distributed
on the beach seemed OK.

00:46:03.390 --> 00:46:08.780 align:middle line:84%
Once you get between competition
between Google and Samsung

00:46:08.780 --> 00:46:12.320 align:middle line:84%
and Apple to sell
modern phones, is it

00:46:12.320 --> 00:46:14.430 align:middle line:90%
really uniform on a triangle?

00:46:14.430 --> 00:46:16.280 align:middle line:90%
Why do we believe that anyway?

00:46:16.280 --> 00:46:17.780 align:middle line:84%
So what people do
instead is they've

00:46:17.780 --> 00:46:21.950 align:middle line:84%
switched to this kind of
utility spectrum, where

00:46:21.950 --> 00:46:24.680 align:middle line:84%
in some sense what
Hotelling was trying to do

00:46:24.680 --> 00:46:28.470 align:middle line:84%
was fit two firms into
a one-dimensional space.

00:46:28.470 --> 00:46:30.840 align:middle line:84%
And fitting two firms into
a one-dimensional space

00:46:30.840 --> 00:46:33.690 align:middle line:84%
was a great idea to
make it more tractable.

00:46:33.690 --> 00:46:38.520 align:middle line:84%
Fitting three firms into
a two-dimensional space,

00:46:38.520 --> 00:46:39.820 align:middle line:90%
it just gets awkward.

00:46:39.820 --> 00:46:42.720 align:middle line:84%
And so the observation is,
why not just stick with

00:46:42.720 --> 00:46:46.290 align:middle line:84%
an n-dimensional space for n
firms instead of an n minus

00:46:46.290 --> 00:46:48.550 align:middle line:90%
1-dimensional space for n firms?

00:46:48.550 --> 00:46:50.790 align:middle line:84%
And so what people do is
assume the utility that

00:46:50.790 --> 00:46:52.650 align:middle line:90%
Firm I gets from by--

00:46:52.650 --> 00:46:54.750 align:middle line:84%
utility that consumer
I gets buying

00:46:54.750 --> 00:47:00.420 align:middle line:84%
from Firm J is vj, which is like
the vertical quality of product

00:47:00.420 --> 00:47:06.240 align:middle line:84%
j, minus alpha times
pj, the price of Firm J,

00:47:06.240 --> 00:47:08.040 align:middle line:90%
plus epsilon ij.

00:47:08.040 --> 00:47:11.760 align:middle line:84%
That's my uniform taste
for how much I like iPhones

00:47:11.760 --> 00:47:13.950 align:middle line:84%
or how much I like
Samsung phones

00:47:13.950 --> 00:47:19.020 align:middle line:84%
or how much I like
Google Pixel phones, OK?

00:47:19.020 --> 00:47:21.270 align:middle line:84%
And then people typically
also actually with n firms

00:47:21.270 --> 00:47:24.420 align:middle line:84%
will now typically go to n
plus 1 dimensional uncertainty.

00:47:24.420 --> 00:47:26.250 align:middle line:84%
And they'll add
epsilon i0, which

00:47:26.250 --> 00:47:28.800 align:middle line:84%
is how much do I like the
outside good of not purchasing

00:47:28.800 --> 00:47:29.740 align:middle line:90%
anything?

00:47:29.740 --> 00:47:32.940 align:middle line:84%
So that could be how
much sentimental value

00:47:32.940 --> 00:47:36.007 align:middle line:84%
do I have for my current
phone, which is often

00:47:36.007 --> 00:47:38.340 align:middle line:84%
the outside good is just not
buying anything and keeping

00:47:38.340 --> 00:47:40.290 align:middle line:90%
the phone that I've already got.

00:47:40.290 --> 00:47:44.498 align:middle line:84%
And people can have
different-- so in some sense,

00:47:44.498 --> 00:47:47.040 align:middle line:84%
in that phone example, I've got
a four-dimensional preference

00:47:47.040 --> 00:47:47.410 align:middle line:90%
space.

00:47:47.410 --> 00:47:48.790 align:middle line:90%
How much do I like new iphones?

00:47:48.790 --> 00:47:50.710 align:middle line:84%
How much do I like
new Samsung phones?

00:47:50.710 --> 00:47:52.440 align:middle line:84%
How much do I like
new Pixel phones?

00:47:52.440 --> 00:47:54.090 align:middle line:84%
And how much do I
like the old phone

00:47:54.090 --> 00:47:55.740 align:middle line:90%
that's already in my pocket?

00:47:55.740 --> 00:47:58.573 align:middle line:84%
And people can have different
views about these things,

00:47:58.573 --> 00:48:00.990 align:middle line:84%
both for taste-based reasons
and like the one in my pocket

00:48:00.990 --> 00:48:06.530 align:middle line:84%
is broken or not broken or
whatever condition it's in, OK?

00:48:06.530 --> 00:48:08.600 align:middle line:84%
Demand in this model
in general case is just

00:48:08.600 --> 00:48:11.510 align:middle line:84%
an n-plus-1-dimensional
integral to figure out.

00:48:11.510 --> 00:48:14.900 align:middle line:84%
The demand for product j,
when these are the prices,

00:48:14.900 --> 00:48:18.780 align:middle line:84%
I just integrate over that
n-plus-1-dimensional space.

00:48:18.780 --> 00:48:23.210 align:middle line:84%
It's just the integral over
the subset of that space, where

00:48:23.210 --> 00:48:26.090 align:middle line:84%
product j gives higher
utility than product

00:48:26.090 --> 00:48:28.440 align:middle line:90%
k for all k different from j.

00:48:28.440 --> 00:48:30.770 align:middle line:84%
And I'm just
integrating the density

00:48:30.770 --> 00:48:33.540 align:middle line:84%
of the joint distribution
of types over that region.

00:48:33.540 --> 00:48:38.180 align:middle line:84%
So it's like here I'm
integrating the density of types

00:48:38.180 --> 00:48:41.190 align:middle line:84%
to find demand for good 1 in
this two-dimensional space.

00:48:41.190 --> 00:48:44.870 align:middle line:84%
I just integrate the density
over this particular region

00:48:44.870 --> 00:48:45.900 align:middle line:90%
of space.

00:48:45.900 --> 00:48:49.640 align:middle line:84%
And so I'm still integrating
over these regions of space

00:48:49.640 --> 00:48:52.610 align:middle line:90%
that look kind of like that.

00:48:52.610 --> 00:48:54.928 align:middle line:84%
Actually, the
two-dimensional model here--

00:48:54.928 --> 00:48:56.720 align:middle line:84%
let me do two products
and no outside good.

00:48:56.720 --> 00:49:02.900 align:middle line:84%
It would just be like I've got
epsilon i1 on this axis, epsilon

00:49:02.900 --> 00:49:05.270 align:middle line:90%
i2 on this axis.

00:49:05.270 --> 00:49:07.890 align:middle line:90%
And I would just be this region.

00:49:07.890 --> 00:49:10.910 align:middle line:90%
So these people buy from good 1.

00:49:10.910 --> 00:49:13.110 align:middle line:90%
These people buy good 2.

00:49:13.110 --> 00:49:16.370 align:middle line:84%
It's just entirely is epsilon
i2 greater than epsilon i1?

00:49:16.370 --> 00:49:18.500 align:middle line:84%
So it's an easier
integral to do, typically,

00:49:18.500 --> 00:49:20.780 align:middle line:84%
than those ones with the
things stuck into a lower

00:49:20.780 --> 00:49:25.040 align:middle line:84%
dimensional space because you're
just integrating over quadrants

00:49:25.040 --> 00:49:25.990 align:middle line:90%
or half spaces.

00:49:25.990 --> 00:49:34.753 align:middle line:90%


00:49:34.753 --> 00:49:37.170 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, in empirical papers,
if people want to do this,

00:49:37.170 --> 00:49:40.172 align:middle line:84%
they're just simulating a bunch
of-- drawing a bunch of epsilons

00:49:40.172 --> 00:49:41.880 align:middle line:84%
at random, figuring
out how many of those

00:49:41.880 --> 00:49:43.530 align:middle line:90%
consumers buy each product.

00:49:43.530 --> 00:49:45.965 align:middle line:84%
That's how you do this,
practically speaking.

00:49:45.965 --> 00:49:48.480 align:middle line:90%


00:49:48.480 --> 00:49:52.140 align:middle line:84%
This model is tractable
in the special case.

00:49:52.140 --> 00:49:56.370 align:middle line:84%
When there's no outside
goods, the v's are all equal,

00:49:56.370 --> 00:49:58.230 align:middle line:90%
and the epsilons are i.i.d.

00:49:58.230 --> 00:50:00.240 align:middle line:90%
with some density f.

00:50:00.240 --> 00:50:06.220 align:middle line:84%
In general, you're trying to
solve a game, an n player game,

00:50:06.220 --> 00:50:09.510 align:middle line:84%
you have to solve an n equations
and n unknowns problem.

00:50:09.510 --> 00:50:13.020 align:middle line:84%
But if you have looking for
a symmetric equilibrium,

00:50:13.020 --> 00:50:15.210 align:middle line:84%
then you're back
to a one equation

00:50:15.210 --> 00:50:16.642 align:middle line:90%
and one unknown problem.

00:50:16.642 --> 00:50:18.600 align:middle line:84%
Because you have a one
unknown equation and one

00:50:18.600 --> 00:50:20.725 align:middle line:84%
unknown problem, I'm just
trying to max-- you know,

00:50:20.725 --> 00:50:24.780 align:middle line:84%
I'm solving the fact
that when is there

00:50:24.780 --> 00:50:27.000 align:middle line:84%
an equilibrium of
a symmetric model

00:50:27.000 --> 00:50:31.410 align:middle line:84%
where all the firms
set price p star.

00:50:31.410 --> 00:50:39.180 align:middle line:84%
That's going to be if p star
belongs to the arg max over p

00:50:39.180 --> 00:50:49.350 align:middle line:84%
of profit of Firm J when it sets
price pj and the other firms

00:50:49.350 --> 00:50:50.435 align:middle line:90%
set price p star.

00:50:50.435 --> 00:50:54.100 align:middle line:90%


00:50:54.100 --> 00:50:56.120 align:middle line:84%
So, sorry, if I
make it symmetric

00:50:56.120 --> 00:50:59.240 align:middle line:84%
so this profit
doesn't depend on j,

00:50:59.240 --> 00:51:01.990 align:middle line:84%
this is just one
equation in one unknown

00:51:01.990 --> 00:51:03.720 align:middle line:90%
that lets me find p star.

00:51:03.720 --> 00:51:06.910 align:middle line:84%
It's just each firm is
maximizing its profit,

00:51:06.910 --> 00:51:09.520 align:middle line:84%
holding everybody else's
profits at p star.

00:51:09.520 --> 00:51:15.520 align:middle line:84%
And so, where asymmetric models
are an n equation problem,

00:51:15.520 --> 00:51:17.220 align:middle line:90%
one equation--

00:51:17.220 --> 00:51:18.790 align:middle line:84%
symmetric equilibria
you can often

00:51:18.790 --> 00:51:21.080 align:middle line:84%
find by just solving one
equation and one unknown.

00:51:21.080 --> 00:51:24.790 align:middle line:84%
And so people often do
that to give insights.

00:51:24.790 --> 00:51:26.560 align:middle line:90%
So let's look at what is the--

00:51:26.560 --> 00:51:32.740 align:middle line:84%
and obviously, the
pi of pj comma p star

00:51:32.740 --> 00:51:47.070 align:middle line:84%
is going to be xj of pj comma
p star times pj minus c.

00:51:47.070 --> 00:51:52.460 align:middle line:84%
So your demand is just price
minus cost times demand,

00:51:52.460 --> 00:51:54.890 align:middle line:84%
like it was in all
the other models.

00:51:54.890 --> 00:51:57.355 align:middle line:90%
OK, what does demand look like?

00:51:57.355 --> 00:52:00.450 align:middle line:90%


00:52:00.450 --> 00:52:04.050 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm thinking about
every other firm is charging

00:52:04.050 --> 00:52:06.780 align:middle line:84%
price p or p star
and I'm considering

00:52:06.780 --> 00:52:13.240 align:middle line:84%
charging my price pj, when is
someone going to buy from me?

00:52:13.240 --> 00:52:18.200 align:middle line:84%
They're going to buy from me
if their preference for me--

00:52:18.200 --> 00:52:23.750 align:middle line:84%
so they're going to buy
from me if epsilon ij is

00:52:23.750 --> 00:52:32.180 align:middle line:84%
at least the maximum k
different from j epsilon ik

00:52:32.180 --> 00:52:38.420 align:middle line:90%
plus pj minus p star.

00:52:38.420 --> 00:52:40.940 align:middle line:84%
If everyone else
is charging p star

00:52:40.940 --> 00:52:44.300 align:middle line:84%
and I charge pj, which
is greater than p star,

00:52:44.300 --> 00:52:46.762 align:middle line:90%
I have to be that much--

00:52:46.762 --> 00:52:49.220 align:middle line:84%
they have to have that much
higher idiosyncratic preference

00:52:49.220 --> 00:52:52.280 align:middle line:84%
for me than they have
for anybody else.

00:52:52.280 --> 00:52:54.050 align:middle line:84%
And if I charge a
lower price, they

00:52:54.050 --> 00:52:55.760 align:middle line:84%
can be this much
lower than their best

00:52:55.760 --> 00:52:58.760 align:middle line:84%
preference for everybody
else and still buy from me.

00:52:58.760 --> 00:53:04.010 align:middle line:84%
So what you get is the
demand for me is this is--

00:53:04.010 --> 00:53:06.440 align:middle line:90%
let's suppose that there--

00:53:06.440 --> 00:53:11.540 align:middle line:84%
suppose that this, I'm going
to call theta, their greatest

00:53:11.540 --> 00:53:14.960 align:middle line:84%
utility they get from buying
from any other firm or greatest

00:53:14.960 --> 00:53:17.900 align:middle line:84%
idiosyncratic taste
for any other firm,

00:53:17.900 --> 00:53:20.810 align:middle line:84%
then they're going
to buy from me--

00:53:20.810 --> 00:53:23.690 align:middle line:84%
this is the probability
that their type is bigger

00:53:23.690 --> 00:53:26.840 align:middle line:90%
than theta plus pj minus p.

00:53:26.840 --> 00:53:28.430 align:middle line:90%
So that's a probability that--

00:53:28.430 --> 00:53:30.830 align:middle line:84%
this is the probability
that epsilon ij

00:53:30.830 --> 00:53:34.220 align:middle line:90%
is bigger than theta plus this.

00:53:34.220 --> 00:53:42.060 align:middle line:84%
And then, this is
the density of theta.

00:53:42.060 --> 00:53:43.456 align:middle line:90%
So if you're choosing--

00:53:43.456 --> 00:53:46.980 align:middle line:90%


00:53:46.980 --> 00:53:52.360 align:middle line:84%
there are n other draws
or n minus 1 other draws,

00:53:52.360 --> 00:53:56.110 align:middle line:84%
there are n minus 1 draws
from some distribution f,

00:53:56.110 --> 00:53:58.900 align:middle line:84%
what's the probability that
the largest of n minus 1

00:53:58.900 --> 00:54:05.200 align:middle line:84%
draws from f is in some tiny
interval theta plus d theta?

00:54:05.200 --> 00:54:07.540 align:middle line:84%
And the probability
that this is-- it's

00:54:07.540 --> 00:54:09.083 align:middle line:90%
an order statistic calculation.

00:54:09.083 --> 00:54:11.500 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if the order
statistics have disappeared from

00:54:11.500 --> 00:54:12.550 align:middle line:90%
[14.]380.

00:54:12.550 --> 00:54:17.980 align:middle line:84%
But so, the density of
the highest of n draws--

00:54:17.980 --> 00:54:21.580 align:middle line:84%
so the probability that someone
is in the highest of n minus 1

00:54:21.580 --> 00:54:25.120 align:middle line:84%
draws in that interval
is you pick some firm

00:54:25.120 --> 00:54:27.810 align:middle line:90%
to be the highest, their value--

00:54:27.810 --> 00:54:31.090 align:middle line:84%
the epsilon for them has to
be in this interval, which

00:54:31.090 --> 00:54:33.610 align:middle line:84%
happens with
probability f of theta

00:54:33.610 --> 00:54:37.570 align:middle line:84%
and all other epsilons have to
be less than that theta, which

00:54:37.570 --> 00:54:40.600 align:middle line:84%
happens with probability capital
F of theta to the n minus 2.

00:54:40.600 --> 00:54:43.060 align:middle line:84%
So this is the density
for the highest

00:54:43.060 --> 00:54:45.650 align:middle line:90%
of the n minus 1 rival draws.

00:54:45.650 --> 00:54:53.228 align:middle line:84%
But so anyway, that's what
my demand looks like, OK?

00:54:53.228 --> 00:54:55.270 align:middle line:84%
If this is what my demand
looks like, then again,

00:54:55.270 --> 00:54:57.270 align:middle line:84%
I'm just going to have
the first-order condition

00:54:57.270 --> 00:55:00.810 align:middle line:90%
that I'm going to have to have.

00:55:00.810 --> 00:55:06.850 align:middle line:84%
p star satisfies the
first-order condition

00:55:06.850 --> 00:55:16.693 align:middle line:84%
d pi j, dpj evaluated at
pj equals p star equals 0.

00:55:16.693 --> 00:55:18.610 align:middle line:84%
And so I'm going to get
first-order condition.

00:55:18.610 --> 00:55:19.985 align:middle line:84%
The first-order
condition is just

00:55:19.985 --> 00:55:25.985 align:middle line:84%
going to look like pj minus c
times dxj dpj plus xj equals 0.

00:55:25.985 --> 00:55:29.280 align:middle line:90%


00:55:29.280 --> 00:55:31.240 align:middle line:84%
And so it turns
out you can just--

00:55:31.240 --> 00:55:32.910 align:middle line:90%
maybe I should do that right.

00:55:32.910 --> 00:55:35.430 align:middle line:84%
Again, so I have this
first-order condition.

00:55:35.430 --> 00:55:41.210 align:middle line:84%
It's going to look
like p star minus c

00:55:41.210 --> 00:55:54.310 align:middle line:84%
times dxj dpj evaluated at p
star plus xj of p star equals 0.

00:55:54.310 --> 00:55:56.080 align:middle line:84%
So that's what the
first-order condition

00:55:56.080 --> 00:55:59.590 align:middle line:84%
is going to look like, as it
has always looked in this class.

00:55:59.590 --> 00:56:03.580 align:middle line:84%
And then the xj
of p star is going

00:56:03.580 --> 00:56:08.140 align:middle line:84%
to be 1 over n in equilibrium
with n symmetric firms.

00:56:08.140 --> 00:56:14.350 align:middle line:84%
So you just get p star minus
c is just equal to minus 1

00:56:14.350 --> 00:56:17.710 align:middle line:90%
over n dxj dpj.

00:56:17.710 --> 00:56:21.760 align:middle line:90%


00:56:21.760 --> 00:56:23.920 align:middle line:84%
One other thing I
meant to mention before

00:56:23.920 --> 00:56:30.220 align:middle line:84%
is look at this utility
function, v minus alpha times p

00:56:30.220 --> 00:56:31.640 align:middle line:90%
plus epsilon.

00:56:31.640 --> 00:56:35.980 align:middle line:84%
Utilities are only defined up
to an affine transformation.

00:56:35.980 --> 00:56:38.860 align:middle line:84%
I think probably hear
that in [14.]121 a lot.

00:56:38.860 --> 00:56:45.640 align:middle line:84%
So you can also think of this as
like v over alpha minus p plus 1

00:56:45.640 --> 00:56:49.120 align:middle line:90%
over alpha times epsilon ij.

00:56:49.120 --> 00:56:53.470 align:middle line:84%
So this model, when people put
in a price coefficient alpha,

00:56:53.470 --> 00:56:57.340 align:middle line:84%
is a lot like the Hotelling
model, where one puts 1

00:56:57.340 --> 00:57:01.490 align:middle line:84%
over alpha where the t used
to be because 1 over alpha

00:57:01.490 --> 00:57:06.050 align:middle line:84%
is like the size of the
transportation cost, size

00:57:06.050 --> 00:57:08.810 align:middle line:84%
of the heterogeneity
relative to having a unit

00:57:08.810 --> 00:57:10.610 align:middle line:90%
coefficient on the price.

00:57:10.610 --> 00:57:14.360 align:middle line:84%
So when people do empirical IO,
they always put an alpha here.

00:57:14.360 --> 00:57:16.850 align:middle line:84%
But you can think of
it as fixing this as 1

00:57:16.850 --> 00:57:21.140 align:middle line:84%
and putting 1 over alpha
in front of the epsilons.

00:57:21.140 --> 00:57:25.580 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, so what do you get
as the markup when you do this?

00:57:25.580 --> 00:57:28.590 align:middle line:84%
What you get as a
markup is price.

00:57:28.590 --> 00:57:35.410 align:middle line:84%
So this is price equals
cost plus negative 1

00:57:35.410 --> 00:57:37.790 align:middle line:90%
over n 1 over dxj dpj.

00:57:37.790 --> 00:57:40.540 align:middle line:90%


00:57:40.540 --> 00:57:42.850 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, the
equilibrium formula

00:57:42.850 --> 00:57:47.770 align:middle line:84%
is price equals c plus
t times something.

00:57:47.770 --> 00:57:52.540 align:middle line:84%
So it's c plus t times 1 over
m of n, and the 1 over m of n

00:57:52.540 --> 00:57:54.646 align:middle line:84%
is just what you get
differentiating--

00:57:54.646 --> 00:57:58.790 align:middle line:90%


00:57:58.790 --> 00:58:00.680 align:middle line:84%
m of n is like what
you get differentiating

00:58:00.680 --> 00:58:02.480 align:middle line:90%
this thing with respect to pj.

00:58:02.480 --> 00:58:05.120 align:middle line:90%


00:58:05.120 --> 00:58:08.210 align:middle line:84%
And you can see it's got
things like f to the n minus 2

00:58:08.210 --> 00:58:11.340 align:middle line:90%
and F and n's in it and stuff.

00:58:11.340 --> 00:58:13.110 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, if you
do the calculation,

00:58:13.110 --> 00:58:15.750 align:middle line:84%
you differentiate on
the integral sign,

00:58:15.750 --> 00:58:17.170 align:middle line:90%
this is what you get.

00:58:17.170 --> 00:58:22.440 align:middle line:84%
So price equals c plus 1 over m
of n times 1 over alpha, where

00:58:22.440 --> 00:58:28.290 align:middle line:84%
m of n depends on the assumed
density of the distribution

00:58:28.290 --> 00:58:31.370 align:middle line:90%
of the epsilons, OK?

00:58:31.370 --> 00:58:32.766 align:middle line:90%
So some corollaries.

00:58:32.766 --> 00:58:35.650 align:middle line:90%


00:58:35.650 --> 00:58:37.930 align:middle line:90%
Suppose F is uniform 0, 1.

00:58:37.930 --> 00:58:43.240 align:middle line:84%
So certainly, Hotelling could
have done, instead of the beach,

00:58:43.240 --> 00:58:45.790 align:middle line:84%
he could have done the
people living on a square,

00:58:45.790 --> 00:58:49.510 align:middle line:84%
where this was
utility epsilon i1.

00:58:49.510 --> 00:58:52.930 align:middle line:90%
This is epsilon i2, OK?

00:58:52.930 --> 00:58:56.320 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that if Hotelling
had done his model on a square,

00:58:56.320 --> 00:59:00.370 align:middle line:84%
he would have found the neat
result that price equals c plus

00:59:00.370 --> 00:59:01.602 align:middle line:90%
1/2t.

00:59:01.602 --> 00:59:04.060 align:middle line:84%
He would have gotten exactly
the same thing except with 1/2

00:59:04.060 --> 00:59:06.040 align:middle line:90%
in it.

00:59:06.040 --> 00:59:08.020 align:middle line:84%
So with the linear
model, you can

00:59:08.020 --> 00:59:11.080 align:middle line:84%
extend this to people living
on a hypercube-- or cube

00:59:11.080 --> 00:59:12.830 align:middle line:90%
or a hypercube or whatever.

00:59:12.830 --> 00:59:15.590 align:middle line:84%
And the formula is
exactly correct,

00:59:15.590 --> 00:59:19.100 align:middle line:84%
that price equals c
plus 1 over n times t.

00:59:19.100 --> 00:59:21.760 align:middle line:84%
So the thing that everyone
loved about Cournot

00:59:21.760 --> 00:59:27.130 align:middle line:84%
is true of this hypercube model
of product differentiation.

00:59:27.130 --> 00:59:30.850 align:middle line:84%
Price is just c plus t,
where t is the length

00:59:30.850 --> 00:59:31.880 align:middle line:90%
of a side of the cube.

00:59:31.880 --> 00:59:33.850 align:middle line:84%
How intense is your
preference for Coke

00:59:33.850 --> 00:59:36.450 align:middle line:90%
versus non-Coke or whatever?

00:59:36.450 --> 00:59:40.310 align:middle line:84%
And things decline
like 1 over n if there

00:59:40.310 --> 00:59:41.440 align:middle line:90%
are n firms in the model.

00:59:41.440 --> 00:59:45.720 align:middle line:90%


00:59:45.720 --> 00:59:47.460 align:middle line:84%
What can we say
more generally going

00:59:47.460 --> 00:59:50.010 align:middle line:84%
outside the uniform
distribution?

00:59:50.010 --> 00:59:53.450 align:middle line:84%
Suppose that uniform
distribution is bounded above.

00:59:53.450 --> 00:59:55.980 align:middle line:84%
Suppose that the
epsilon is bounded above

00:59:55.980 --> 00:59:57.960 align:middle line:90%
or that the tails are thin.

00:59:57.960 --> 01:00:00.240 align:middle line:84%
Tails are thin like
the normal distribution

01:00:00.240 --> 01:00:02.310 align:middle line:84%
in this sense of
limit as epsilon

01:00:02.310 --> 01:00:07.020 align:middle line:84%
goes to infinity F prime over
F goes to negative infinity.

01:00:07.020 --> 01:00:12.350 align:middle line:84%
Then, as the number of firms
goes up, prices drop to cost.

01:00:12.350 --> 01:00:15.420 align:middle line:84%
So if we take
bounded distributions

01:00:15.420 --> 01:00:18.540 align:middle line:84%
or we take distributions
that are not bounded but have

01:00:18.540 --> 01:00:21.210 align:middle line:84%
very thin tails, then
you do get the result

01:00:21.210 --> 01:00:24.630 align:middle line:84%
that competition
drives prices to 0.

01:00:24.630 --> 01:00:26.890 align:middle line:84%
Does that happen for
all distributions?

01:00:26.890 --> 01:00:28.270 align:middle line:90%
The answer is no.

01:00:28.270 --> 01:00:30.900 align:middle line:84%
Most important example
where that doesn't happen

01:00:30.900 --> 01:00:33.210 align:middle line:84%
is the logit error
distribution, which people

01:00:33.210 --> 01:00:36.310 align:middle line:84%
often assume because it
makes algebra easier.

01:00:36.310 --> 01:00:38.590 align:middle line:84%
But it turns out for
the logit model--

01:00:38.590 --> 01:00:42.690 align:middle line:84%
so this is the logit
cdf, where gamma

01:00:42.690 --> 01:00:46.520 align:middle line:84%
is the Euler-Mascheroni
constant, like 0.577.

01:00:46.520 --> 01:00:49.600 align:middle line:84%
So in the logit model,
what you actually get

01:00:49.600 --> 01:00:55.480 align:middle line:84%
is price equals cost plus
some constant times 1

01:00:55.480 --> 01:00:59.050 align:middle line:84%
plus 1 over n minus
1 times 1 over alpha.

01:00:59.050 --> 01:01:08.210 align:middle line:84%
So as n goes to infinity, p star
is going to c plus 1 over alpha.

01:01:08.210 --> 01:01:11.085 align:middle line:90%
So in the logit model, prices--

01:01:11.085 --> 01:01:11.960 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:01:11.960 --> 01:01:13.642 align:middle line:90%
GLENN ELLISON: What?

01:01:13.642 --> 01:01:14.600 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: k over alpha?

01:01:14.600 --> 01:01:16.142 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: Oh,
yes, k over alpha.

01:01:16.142 --> 01:01:21.165 align:middle line:90%
Thank you, where k is some--

01:01:21.165 --> 01:01:22.040 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:01:22.040 --> 01:01:22.940 align:middle line:90%
GLENN ELLISON: k, it's--

01:01:22.940 --> 01:01:23.195 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: It's some arbitrary--

01:01:23.195 --> 01:01:24.612 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON:
It's some constant.

01:01:24.612 --> 01:01:25.790 align:middle line:90%
I forget exactly.

01:01:25.790 --> 01:01:27.660 align:middle line:84%
You can think of
it as like 0.2378.

01:01:27.660 --> 01:01:30.300 align:middle line:84%
I forget what the number
is, but it's just a number.

01:01:30.300 --> 01:01:31.040 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:01:31.040 --> 01:01:35.900 align:middle line:84%
So in the logit model, you
can compute what it is.

01:01:35.900 --> 01:01:39.020 align:middle line:90%
Prices are just like c plus--

01:01:39.020 --> 01:01:40.740 align:middle line:84%
as the number of firms
goes to infinity,

01:01:40.740 --> 01:01:44.210 align:middle line:84%
competition doesn't
eliminate markups.

01:01:44.210 --> 01:01:47.240 align:middle line:90%
And how is this plausible?

01:01:47.240 --> 01:01:51.920 align:middle line:84%
The idea is that these models
have a somewhat unreasonable

01:01:51.920 --> 01:01:57.500 align:middle line:84%
assumption that I snuck
over on you, which was that,

01:01:57.500 --> 01:01:59.840 align:middle line:84%
as I said, in two
dimensions, it's

01:01:59.840 --> 01:02:03.917 align:middle line:84%
like, how much do I like iphones
versus do I like Samsung phones?

01:02:03.917 --> 01:02:05.750 align:middle line:84%
It seems like, OK, it's
reasonable to assume

01:02:05.750 --> 01:02:07.917 align:middle line:84%
there are people with those
two separate differences

01:02:07.917 --> 01:02:08.660 align:middle line:90%
of preferences.

01:02:08.660 --> 01:02:11.720 align:middle line:84%
But what I snuck
over on you is that--

01:02:11.720 --> 01:02:14.870 align:middle line:84%
here I said it-- epsilons
are i.i.d., independent

01:02:14.870 --> 01:02:16.520 align:middle line:90%
and identically distributed.

01:02:16.520 --> 01:02:20.030 align:middle line:84%
The more products you have,
it gets harder and harder

01:02:20.030 --> 01:02:23.330 align:middle line:84%
to say it's really plausible
that my taste for a BMW

01:02:23.330 --> 01:02:25.970 align:middle line:84%
and a Mercedes and a
whatever are all completely

01:02:25.970 --> 01:02:27.650 align:middle line:90%
independent of each other.

01:02:27.650 --> 01:02:29.480 align:middle line:84%
As the number of
firms gets large,

01:02:29.480 --> 01:02:31.500 align:middle line:84%
the products get awfully
similar to each other.

01:02:31.500 --> 01:02:34.100 align:middle line:84%
So if you're a person who
likes Mercedes styling,

01:02:34.100 --> 01:02:36.230 align:middle line:84%
you probably also
like BMW styling.

01:02:36.230 --> 01:02:40.010 align:middle line:84%
And if you're one who
likes Toyota Lexus styling,

01:02:40.010 --> 01:02:43.800 align:middle line:84%
maybe you also like Acura
styling, or whatever.

01:02:43.800 --> 01:02:46.640 align:middle line:84%
So, in some sense, what the
logit model is assuming,

01:02:46.640 --> 01:02:49.250 align:middle line:84%
if you assume we keep having
this-- every new product has

01:02:49.250 --> 01:02:53.810 align:middle line:84%
this completely new
idiosyncratic reaction to it,

01:02:53.810 --> 01:02:55.800 align:middle line:84%
suppose there are a
million products out there

01:02:55.800 --> 01:02:57.330 align:middle line:84%
and you tell me,
OK, yeah, you're

01:02:57.330 --> 01:02:59.400 align:middle line:84%
one of a million
products in the market.

01:02:59.400 --> 01:03:01.680 align:middle line:84%
But this person likes
your product better

01:03:01.680 --> 01:03:03.850 align:middle line:90%
than every other product.

01:03:03.850 --> 01:03:05.620 align:middle line:90%
Well, to think about--

01:03:05.620 --> 01:03:08.470 align:middle line:84%
the markups depend
on what this dxj dpj.

01:03:08.470 --> 01:03:12.112 align:middle line:84%
What do I conclude
when I've just

01:03:12.112 --> 01:03:13.570 align:middle line:84%
been told they like
my product more

01:03:13.570 --> 01:03:15.200 align:middle line:90%
than everybody else's product?

01:03:15.200 --> 01:03:16.040 align:middle line:90%
I'm like, wow.

01:03:16.040 --> 01:03:18.190 align:middle line:90%
They like my product a lot, OK?

01:03:18.190 --> 01:03:24.850 align:middle line:84%
Now, in the hypercube
model, if the epsilon ij

01:03:24.850 --> 01:03:29.940 align:middle line:84%
is distributed uniform
0, 1, I'm like,

01:03:29.940 --> 01:03:31.690 align:middle line:84%
they like me best out
of a million things.

01:03:31.690 --> 01:03:32.800 align:middle line:90%
It must be my--

01:03:32.800 --> 01:03:37.480 align:middle line:84%
utility for me is like
0.999999 something or other,

01:03:37.480 --> 01:03:39.280 align:middle line:84%
or they wouldn't have
been buying from me.

01:03:39.280 --> 01:03:41.020 align:middle line:84%
But while they
like me this much,

01:03:41.020 --> 01:03:46.400 align:middle line:84%
there's also somebody else who
they like 0.999998 or something.

01:03:46.400 --> 01:03:48.340 align:middle line:84%
And so I think the
amount by which

01:03:48.340 --> 01:03:51.530 align:middle line:84%
they like my product over
anybody else's product is tiny.

01:03:51.530 --> 01:03:54.550 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, my demand is
very price sensitive.

01:03:54.550 --> 01:03:57.760 align:middle line:84%
And if I don't price really
low, they're going to switch.

01:03:57.760 --> 01:04:00.440 align:middle line:84%
So I have this incredibly
price-sensitive demand.

01:04:00.440 --> 01:04:02.357 align:middle line:90%
But if you have a--

01:04:02.357 --> 01:04:03.190 align:middle line:90%
maybe you know this.

01:04:03.190 --> 01:04:06.190 align:middle line:84%
If you have an
exponential distribution

01:04:06.190 --> 01:04:09.470 align:middle line:84%
and you're told that you're
the largest of n exponentials,

01:04:09.470 --> 01:04:12.400 align:middle line:84%
how far are you above
the next highest draw?

01:04:12.400 --> 01:04:13.600 align:middle line:90%
The answer is 1.

01:04:13.600 --> 01:04:15.940 align:middle line:84%
That's a property of the
exponential distribution,

01:04:15.940 --> 01:04:18.370 align:middle line:84%
that if you're the
highest of n draws

01:04:18.370 --> 01:04:23.350 align:middle line:84%
from a standard exponential,
your posterior for their value

01:04:23.350 --> 01:04:24.280 align:middle line:90%
for you is n.

01:04:24.280 --> 01:04:25.780 align:middle line:84%
And your posterior
for the product--

01:04:25.780 --> 01:04:27.280 align:middle line:84%
their value for the
next highest one

01:04:27.280 --> 01:04:29.550 align:middle line:90%
has high expectation n minus 1.

01:04:29.550 --> 01:04:33.670 align:middle line:84%
So you think you
have one of space

01:04:33.670 --> 01:04:35.950 align:middle line:90%
to price relative to your rival.

01:04:35.950 --> 01:04:38.200 align:middle line:84%
And so with an exponential
distribution, we actually--

01:04:38.200 --> 01:04:39.640 align:middle line:90%
you get--

01:04:39.640 --> 01:04:42.490 align:middle line:84%
exponential distribution,
you're going to get like p star

01:04:42.490 --> 01:04:45.430 align:middle line:84%
equals c plus t doesn't
depend on n at all.

01:04:45.430 --> 01:04:47.800 align:middle line:84%
The more exponential
draws that are out there,

01:04:47.800 --> 01:04:49.180 align:middle line:84%
they're just,
yeah, they probably

01:04:49.180 --> 01:04:51.460 align:middle line:84%
like me a lot more
than somebody else.

01:04:51.460 --> 01:04:52.610 align:middle line:90%
They like me a million.

01:04:52.610 --> 01:04:55.810 align:middle line:90%
They like the next guy 999,999.

01:04:55.810 --> 01:04:58.060 align:middle line:84%
Logit isn't quite
like the exponential,

01:04:58.060 --> 01:05:01.150 align:middle line:84%
but it turns out to be just
very similar to the exponential

01:05:01.150 --> 01:05:03.910 align:middle line:90%
and that approximately--

01:05:03.910 --> 01:05:08.400 align:middle line:84%
it ends up with an
approximately constant markup.

01:05:08.400 --> 01:05:10.590 align:middle line:84%
And then markup
depends on what weight

01:05:10.590 --> 01:05:12.810 align:middle line:84%
you stick in front
of the epsilon ij

01:05:12.810 --> 01:05:14.960 align:middle line:84%
in the logit model,
the 1 over alpha.

01:05:14.960 --> 01:05:17.640 align:middle line:90%


01:05:17.640 --> 01:05:19.597 align:middle line:84%
And there are
distributions for which--

01:05:19.597 --> 01:05:21.930 align:middle line:84%
if you take a really fat-tailed
distribution, one that's

01:05:21.930 --> 01:05:23.850 align:middle line:84%
fatter tailed than
the exponential,

01:05:23.850 --> 01:05:26.950 align:middle line:84%
prices can go up as
n goes to infinity.

01:05:26.950 --> 01:05:31.270 align:middle line:84%
And I don't know that we
think that's often realistic.

01:05:31.270 --> 01:05:34.000 align:middle line:84%
Where do these-- you keep
getting these independent tastes

01:05:34.000 --> 01:05:34.900 align:middle line:90%
from?

01:05:34.900 --> 01:05:38.410 align:middle line:84%
But in these models,
it's there, and you

01:05:38.410 --> 01:05:40.690 align:middle line:90%
should be aware of it, OK?

01:05:40.690 --> 01:05:43.340 align:middle line:90%


01:05:43.340 --> 01:05:45.060 align:middle line:90%
Questions on that?

01:05:45.060 --> 01:05:47.800 align:middle line:84%
So, OK, I think that's
what I had to say.

01:05:47.800 --> 01:05:51.890 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so I think I'm
going to skip this slide.

01:05:51.890 --> 01:05:54.230 align:middle line:84%
One thing I wanted to
say in this slide is

01:05:54.230 --> 01:05:56.330 align:middle line:84%
suppose a student
comes to me and says,

01:05:56.330 --> 01:05:58.430 align:middle line:84%
I want to write a
paper about mergers

01:05:58.430 --> 01:06:03.650 align:middle line:84%
and the effect mergers are
having on markups in industry x.

01:06:03.650 --> 01:06:07.350 align:middle line:84%
This is a know-your-theory
cautionary tale.

01:06:07.350 --> 01:06:10.070 align:middle line:84%
If a student says, OK, I'm
going to estimate logit demand

01:06:10.070 --> 01:06:12.560 align:middle line:84%
in this model and see what's
happened when you merge

01:06:12.560 --> 01:06:14.550 align:middle line:84%
from five firms
down to four firms

01:06:14.550 --> 01:06:18.025 align:middle line:84%
if phone maker A buys
phone maker B, or whatever.

01:06:18.025 --> 01:06:20.345 align:middle line:90%


01:06:20.345 --> 01:06:22.220 align:middle line:84%
So you can estimate your
logit model and say,

01:06:22.220 --> 01:06:23.887 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to allow
the price sensitivity

01:06:23.887 --> 01:06:30.092 align:middle line:84%
to vary and have it be v minus
alpha p plus epsilon ij utility.

01:06:30.092 --> 01:06:31.550 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to
estimate the alpha

01:06:31.550 --> 01:06:33.720 align:middle line:84%
and see how price
sensitive things are.

01:06:33.720 --> 01:06:35.150 align:middle line:84%
And then when I find out how
price sensitive things are,

01:06:35.150 --> 01:06:36.608 align:middle line:84%
see what the merger
is going to do.

01:06:36.608 --> 01:06:39.510 align:middle line:90%


01:06:39.510 --> 01:06:40.920 align:middle line:84%
If you then use
that model to say

01:06:40.920 --> 01:06:44.010 align:middle line:84%
when you go from five to four
firms, how do markups go up?

01:06:44.010 --> 01:06:46.980 align:middle line:84%
The answer is markups
go up by 1.067.

01:06:46.980 --> 01:06:51.990 align:middle line:84%
With five symmetric firms, the
markup is c plus k over thing

01:06:51.990 --> 01:06:53.230 align:middle line:90%
times 5/4.

01:06:53.230 --> 01:06:58.300 align:middle line:84%
When you go to four firms, it's
c plus k over alpha times 4/3.

01:06:58.300 --> 01:07:01.410 align:middle line:84%
So on the question of how
do markups change when

01:07:01.410 --> 01:07:03.510 align:middle line:84%
you go from four
firms to three firms--

01:07:03.510 --> 01:07:07.200 align:middle line:84%
five firms to four
firms, the answer

01:07:07.200 --> 01:07:09.873 align:middle line:84%
is-- that question
has an answer that's

01:07:09.873 --> 01:07:11.790 align:middle line:84%
independent of all the
parameters you estimate

01:07:11.790 --> 01:07:12.760 align:middle line:90%
in the model.

01:07:12.760 --> 01:07:14.650 align:middle line:90%
And the answer is just this.

01:07:14.650 --> 01:07:20.160 align:middle line:84%
And so if you do want to
estimate how mergers would

01:07:20.160 --> 01:07:24.420 align:middle line:84%
affect markups in models with
horizontal differentiation,

01:07:24.420 --> 01:07:30.340 align:middle line:84%
you have to make sure you're
correctly estimating this object

01:07:30.340 --> 01:07:34.470 align:middle line:90%
dxj dpi versus dxi dpi.

01:07:34.470 --> 01:07:38.400 align:middle line:84%
And the models, like
the logit model,

01:07:38.400 --> 01:07:40.900 align:middle line:84%
can have built-in assumptions
about what those things are.

01:07:40.900 --> 01:07:42.960 align:middle line:84%
And if these things
both just have

01:07:42.960 --> 01:07:45.690 align:middle line:84%
an alpha in them and
those alphas cancel,

01:07:45.690 --> 01:07:47.790 align:middle line:84%
you're not actually
estimating what

01:07:47.790 --> 01:07:51.035 align:middle line:90%
you think you're estimating.

01:07:51.035 --> 01:07:52.660 align:middle line:84%
But I didn't want to
take time on that.

01:07:52.660 --> 01:07:58.210 align:middle line:84%
So going to more
modern pictures,

01:07:58.210 --> 01:08:00.580 align:middle line:84%
I stuck Jidong's
picture on this slide.

01:08:00.580 --> 01:08:07.200 align:middle line:84%
So discuss a recent paper
discussing the effects

01:08:07.200 --> 01:08:10.080 align:middle line:90%
of bundling on competition.

01:08:10.080 --> 01:08:16.890 align:middle line:84%
So the motivation
for this paper is

01:08:16.890 --> 01:08:21.569 align:middle line:84%
there are many firms where
there is this antitrust concern

01:08:21.569 --> 01:08:25.590 align:middle line:84%
about firms bundling and selling
their products together instead

01:08:25.590 --> 01:08:27.870 align:middle line:84%
of selling their
products separately.

01:08:27.870 --> 01:08:30.912 align:middle line:84%
So firms, for
instance, if you're--

01:08:30.912 --> 01:08:32.620 align:middle line:84%
maybe you think they
don't really matter.

01:08:32.620 --> 01:08:37.590 align:middle line:84%
But Apple is selling phones
and they're selling earbuds.

01:08:37.590 --> 01:08:39.282 align:middle line:84%
And you can think
of, I can design

01:08:39.282 --> 01:08:41.490 align:middle line:84%
the phones and the earbuds
so they only work together

01:08:41.490 --> 01:08:43.120 align:middle line:84%
and have to be
bought as a bundle.

01:08:43.120 --> 01:08:46.540 align:middle line:84%
Or I can design the phones and
earbuds so they work separately.

01:08:46.540 --> 01:08:49.080 align:middle line:84%
And I can be competing
with Samsung separately

01:08:49.080 --> 01:08:52.200 align:middle line:84%
in the phone market and
separately in the earbud market.

01:08:52.200 --> 01:08:53.819 align:middle line:84%
With operating
systems, this often

01:08:53.819 --> 01:08:57.450 align:middle line:84%
comes up as what features do I
build into an operating system

01:08:57.450 --> 01:09:00.060 align:middle line:84%
so that people have to buy
the bundle of the operating

01:09:00.060 --> 01:09:02.460 align:middle line:84%
system containing these 37
different components, all

01:09:02.460 --> 01:09:03.240 align:middle line:90%
from me?

01:09:03.240 --> 01:09:05.490 align:middle line:84%
Or do I allow
plug-and-play compatibility

01:09:05.490 --> 01:09:09.200 align:middle line:84%
so we have separate
competition product by product?

01:09:09.200 --> 01:09:10.439 align:middle line:90%
OK?

01:09:10.439 --> 01:09:14.490 align:middle line:84%
OK, and so this is a
potential antitrust remedy

01:09:14.490 --> 01:09:16.500 align:middle line:84%
and industries with
large market power

01:09:16.500 --> 01:09:20.100 align:middle line:84%
is forcing them to
be compatible and not

01:09:20.100 --> 01:09:23.580 align:middle line:84%
use proprietary
connectors or whatever

01:09:23.580 --> 01:09:25.090 align:middle line:90%
to extend their monopolies.

01:09:25.090 --> 01:09:31.010 align:middle line:84%
And so Joe's paper is about this
question of, when will markups--

01:09:31.010 --> 01:09:33.899 align:middle line:84%
will bundling products
together make markups higher?

01:09:33.899 --> 01:09:35.160 align:middle line:90%
Will it make markups lower?

01:09:35.160 --> 01:09:37.790 align:middle line:84%
How will it affect
consumer utility?

01:09:37.790 --> 01:09:43.460 align:middle line:84%
So in his model, you
have these n firms,

01:09:43.460 --> 01:09:47.810 align:middle line:84%
and the n firms all sell
m distinct products.

01:09:47.810 --> 01:09:50.529 align:middle line:84%
And he looks at utilities
of the following form.

01:09:50.529 --> 01:09:56.120 align:middle line:84%
You get utility v minus
pjm plus epsilon ijm

01:09:56.120 --> 01:10:00.740 align:middle line:90%
if you buy product j--

01:10:00.740 --> 01:10:05.050 align:middle line:84%
if you buy-- sorry--
product m from Firm J.

01:10:05.050 --> 01:10:08.910 align:middle line:84%
And he compares two
separate models.

01:10:08.910 --> 01:10:13.580 align:middle line:84%
One model is where the
firms compete separately

01:10:13.580 --> 01:10:15.810 align:middle line:90%
in this whole matrix of prices.

01:10:15.810 --> 01:10:22.610 align:middle line:84%
So Firm 1 sets prices
p11, p12, up to p1m.

01:10:22.610 --> 01:10:27.200 align:middle line:84%
And then Firm 2 sets
prices p21 up to p2m.

01:10:27.200 --> 01:10:28.760 align:middle line:90%
And then Firm 3--

01:10:28.760 --> 01:10:32.330 align:middle line:84%
I'll just have three firms--
post its bundle of prices p3m.

01:10:32.330 --> 01:10:36.680 align:middle line:84%
He compares that to a bundled
system where the firms just

01:10:36.680 --> 01:10:39.570 align:middle line:84%
design their products to be
all mutually incompatible.

01:10:39.570 --> 01:10:46.050 align:middle line:84%
So Firm 1 just sets
a price capital p1.

01:10:46.050 --> 01:10:48.390 align:middle line:84%
Firm 2 sets of
price, capital P2.

01:10:48.390 --> 01:10:51.580 align:middle line:84%
And Firm 3 sets of price
capital P3 for the bundle.

01:10:51.580 --> 01:10:54.537 align:middle line:84%
So this is the bundle of getting
all of these goods from me.

01:10:54.537 --> 01:10:57.120 align:middle line:84%
This is the bundle of getting
all of these things from Firm 2.

01:10:57.120 --> 01:10:58.620 align:middle line:84%
And this is the bundle of
getting all of these things

01:10:58.620 --> 01:10:59.770 align:middle line:90%
from Firm 3.

01:10:59.770 --> 01:11:03.060 align:middle line:84%
And he thinks about which
results in greater consumer

01:11:03.060 --> 01:11:07.620 align:middle line:84%
welfare and greater profits,
having unbundled pricing

01:11:07.620 --> 01:11:09.125 align:middle line:90%
or having bundled pricing?

01:11:09.125 --> 01:11:12.210 align:middle line:90%


01:11:12.210 --> 01:11:19.770 align:middle line:84%
OK, first observation is when do
consumers prefer buying bundle 1

01:11:19.770 --> 01:11:21.300 align:middle line:90%
from bundle 2?

01:11:21.300 --> 01:11:25.960 align:middle line:84%
Well, that's if m
times v minus the price

01:11:25.960 --> 01:11:28.750 align:middle line:84%
of the bundle plus the
sum across all products

01:11:28.750 --> 01:11:31.600 align:middle line:84%
within the bundle
of epsilon ijm is

01:11:31.600 --> 01:11:35.980 align:middle line:84%
better than buying mv minus
the price of bundle k times

01:11:35.980 --> 01:11:39.940 align:middle line:84%
the sum over all things in
the bundle of epsilon ikm.

01:11:39.940 --> 01:11:42.850 align:middle line:84%
And again, normalizing
this by dividing

01:11:42.850 --> 01:11:44.560 align:middle line:90%
by the number of firms--

01:11:44.560 --> 01:11:46.900 align:middle line:90%
so if I convert this to--

01:11:46.900 --> 01:11:48.700 align:middle line:90%
this is m products.

01:11:48.700 --> 01:11:52.515 align:middle line:84%
So p1 over m I'm going to think
of as the per product price.

01:11:52.515 --> 01:11:55.810 align:middle line:90%


01:11:55.810 --> 01:11:59.770 align:middle line:84%
This is what's going to be
equivalent to p11 or something.

01:11:59.770 --> 01:12:02.650 align:middle line:84%
How much are we charging
per product in the bundle?

01:12:02.650 --> 01:12:11.700 align:middle line:84%
So I like this one better if
and only if v minus 1 over mpj

01:12:11.700 --> 01:12:16.870 align:middle line:84%
plus 1 over m the sum of
epsilon ijm is bigger than this.

01:12:16.870 --> 01:12:20.890 align:middle line:84%
So basically, bundling is like
converting the model where

01:12:20.890 --> 01:12:29.960 align:middle line:84%
you have utilities epsilon
i1 up to epsilon n1

01:12:29.960 --> 01:12:35.950 align:middle line:84%
into a model where you have
1 over m sum of epsilon i1

01:12:35.950 --> 01:12:40.250 align:middle line:84%
and 1 over m sum of
epsilon im, right?

01:12:40.250 --> 01:12:42.700 align:middle line:84%
You're just basically
comparing the model

01:12:42.700 --> 01:12:45.610 align:middle line:84%
with utilities
from the original--

01:12:45.610 --> 01:12:47.890 align:middle line:84%
random draws from the
original distribution

01:12:47.890 --> 01:12:50.290 align:middle line:84%
to the model with
utilities that are random

01:12:50.290 --> 01:12:52.563 align:middle line:90%
draws from this distribution.

01:12:52.563 --> 01:12:53.980 align:middle line:84%
So that's what I
do when I bundle,

01:12:53.980 --> 01:12:55.360 align:middle line:84%
is I just change
the distribution

01:12:55.360 --> 01:12:57.490 align:middle line:84%
of idiosyncratic
consumer preferences

01:12:57.490 --> 01:13:00.300 align:middle line:90%
and make it an average of these.

01:13:00.300 --> 01:13:04.620 align:middle line:84%
And obviously, the
central limit theorem

01:13:04.620 --> 01:13:06.930 align:middle line:84%
is going to tell us that
as m goes to infinity,

01:13:06.930 --> 01:13:09.328 align:middle line:84%
these things are going
to have smaller--

01:13:09.328 --> 01:13:11.370 align:middle line:84%
this is going to have
smaller variance than that.

01:13:11.370 --> 01:13:13.920 align:middle line:84%
And it's going to look
approximately normal as m goes

01:13:13.920 --> 01:13:16.830 align:middle line:84%
to infinity, but normal
with a smaller variance

01:13:16.830 --> 01:13:17.810 align:middle line:90%
than the first one had.

01:13:17.810 --> 01:13:21.430 align:middle line:90%


01:13:21.430 --> 01:13:24.610 align:middle line:84%
OK, so let's think about
whether prices are higher.

01:13:24.610 --> 01:13:27.880 align:middle line:84%
Again, same price,
first-order condition I've

01:13:27.880 --> 01:13:29.330 align:middle line:90%
done over and over again.

01:13:29.330 --> 01:13:32.500 align:middle line:84%
So the first-order condition
for price competition

01:13:32.500 --> 01:13:38.530 align:middle line:84%
is pj minus c times
minus dxj dpj equals xj.

01:13:38.530 --> 01:13:41.140 align:middle line:84%
And again, if I'm looking
for a symmetric equilibrium

01:13:41.140 --> 01:13:43.090 align:middle line:84%
of this model,
this is going to be

01:13:43.090 --> 01:13:48.825 align:middle line:84%
p star minus c times minus
dxj dpj equals 1 over n.

01:13:48.825 --> 01:13:51.860 align:middle line:90%


01:13:51.860 --> 01:13:55.970 align:middle line:90%
And what is dxj dpj going to be?

01:13:55.970 --> 01:13:59.960 align:middle line:84%
dxj dpj is just the
density of consumers

01:13:59.960 --> 01:14:04.160 align:middle line:84%
who have utility for
me equal to the maximum

01:14:04.160 --> 01:14:07.243 align:middle line:84%
of their idiosyncratic
reaction to me

01:14:07.243 --> 01:14:09.410 align:middle line:84%
equal to the maximum of
their idiosyncratic reaction

01:14:09.410 --> 01:14:11.740 align:middle line:90%
to everybody else.

01:14:11.740 --> 01:14:21.680 align:middle line:84%
And so, fixing n, this
first-order condition-- where--

01:14:21.680 --> 01:14:23.900 align:middle line:84%
I don't have it
written down anymore.

01:14:23.900 --> 01:14:29.090 align:middle line:84%
So my first-order
condition was p star

01:14:29.090 --> 01:14:36.000 align:middle line:84%
equals c minus 1 over
n times 1 over dxj dpj.

01:14:36.000 --> 01:14:39.650 align:middle line:90%
All right, let me do this, OK?

01:14:39.650 --> 01:14:41.510 align:middle line:84%
That's what my first-order
conditions always

01:14:41.510 --> 01:14:42.750 align:middle line:90%
keep looking like.

01:14:42.750 --> 01:14:47.870 align:middle line:84%
So equilibrium prices are
low if this density is high.

01:14:47.870 --> 01:14:51.290 align:middle line:84%
And equilibrium prices are
high, if this density is low.

01:14:51.290 --> 01:14:54.260 align:middle line:84%
So in this dxj
dpj is the density

01:14:54.260 --> 01:14:58.930 align:middle line:84%
of the set of consumers
who have my epsilon just

01:14:58.930 --> 01:15:01.990 align:middle line:90%
about everybody else's epsilon.

01:15:01.990 --> 01:15:05.710 align:middle line:84%
Notice that the distribution
of sum of the epsilons,

01:15:05.710 --> 01:15:07.390 align:middle line:84%
it's more concentrated
about its mean.

01:15:07.390 --> 01:15:08.550 align:middle line:90%
It's thinner in the tails.

01:15:08.550 --> 01:15:15.150 align:middle line:90%


01:15:15.150 --> 01:15:17.580 align:middle line:84%
OK, so consumer
surplus in this model

01:15:17.580 --> 01:15:20.920 align:middle line:84%
depends on two main welfare
effects for consumers.

01:15:20.920 --> 01:15:22.510 align:middle line:90%
One is price levels.

01:15:22.510 --> 01:15:25.680 align:middle line:90%
Consumers like low prices.

01:15:25.680 --> 01:15:28.980 align:middle line:84%
And there's less deadweight
loss when prices are low.

01:15:28.980 --> 01:15:32.500 align:middle line:84%
The other thing that welfare
depends on is match quality.

01:15:32.500 --> 01:15:35.340 align:middle line:84%
Consumers are better if they're
getting their favorite phone

01:15:35.340 --> 01:15:38.190 align:middle line:84%
and their favorite earbuds
and their favorite charger

01:15:38.190 --> 01:15:41.437 align:middle line:84%
than if they're getting
their favorite of one

01:15:41.437 --> 01:15:43.020 align:middle line:84%
and their second
favorite of the other

01:15:43.020 --> 01:15:44.970 align:middle line:84%
and their third
favorite of another.

01:15:44.970 --> 01:15:47.040 align:middle line:84%
So the match quality
is are consumers

01:15:47.040 --> 01:15:51.540 align:middle line:84%
getting what's the best product
for them on every subcomponent?

01:15:51.540 --> 01:15:55.610 align:middle line:84%
And then there's the price level
thing that they care about.

01:15:55.610 --> 01:15:57.180 align:middle line:90%
OK, so what's the result?

01:15:57.180 --> 01:16:00.620 align:middle line:84%
So first result in the
paper is that bundling

01:16:00.620 --> 01:16:04.130 align:middle line:84%
reduces per good
prices when n equals 2.

01:16:04.130 --> 01:16:08.810 align:middle line:84%
So if you have two firms
and they bundle their goods

01:16:08.810 --> 01:16:12.565 align:middle line:84%
together, equilibrium per
product prices go down.

01:16:12.565 --> 01:16:16.300 align:middle line:90%


01:16:16.300 --> 01:16:21.360 align:middle line:84%
Noting prices go down, meaning
that the firms make less profit.

01:16:21.360 --> 01:16:24.680 align:middle line:90%
And why is that?

01:16:24.680 --> 01:16:31.000 align:middle line:84%
The answer is that density
of 1 over m epsilon im

01:16:31.000 --> 01:16:33.130 align:middle line:90%
becomes more concentrated.

01:16:33.130 --> 01:16:36.040 align:middle line:84%
When n equals 2,
what we have is just

01:16:36.040 --> 01:16:40.040 align:middle line:84%
what's the probability that ui1
minus ui2 is less than delta?

01:16:40.040 --> 01:16:42.940 align:middle line:84%
And that's just the
probability that we're close

01:16:42.940 --> 01:16:44.760 align:middle line:90%
to the 45-degree line.

01:16:44.760 --> 01:16:50.400 align:middle line:84%
That is that my epsilon for
Firm 1 is within epsilon of my--

01:16:50.400 --> 01:16:53.420 align:middle line:84%
I'm sorry, within, let's
say, this small delta.

01:16:53.420 --> 01:16:55.810 align:middle line:84%
What's the probability that
my preference for Firm 1

01:16:55.810 --> 01:16:58.070 align:middle line:84%
and my preference for
Firm 2 are within delta?

01:16:58.070 --> 01:17:00.610 align:middle line:84%
Well, if you take the
distribution of tastes that used

01:17:00.610 --> 01:17:04.210 align:middle line:90%
to be uniform and you sort of--

01:17:04.210 --> 01:17:05.710 align:middle line:84%
like in the uniform
distribution,

01:17:05.710 --> 01:17:07.100 align:middle line:90%
this thing is not all that big.

01:17:07.100 --> 01:17:08.770 align:middle line:84%
But then you add
n goods, and you

01:17:08.770 --> 01:17:11.880 align:middle line:84%
make this distribution
look like this.

01:17:11.880 --> 01:17:14.110 align:middle line:84%
Almost everybody has a
preference of exactly 1/2

01:17:14.110 --> 01:17:15.480 align:middle line:90%
for both products.

01:17:15.480 --> 01:17:17.800 align:middle line:84%
Then, once they have a utility
of exactly 1/2 for both

01:17:17.800 --> 01:17:20.770 align:middle line:84%
products, they have a huge
probability they're within some

01:17:20.770 --> 01:17:22.310 align:middle line:90%
tiny delta of each other.

01:17:22.310 --> 01:17:25.060 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, price competition
becomes very intense.

01:17:25.060 --> 01:17:31.360 align:middle line:84%
So when n equals two, it
turns out that whatever m is,

01:17:31.360 --> 01:17:35.220 align:middle line:84%
bundling just always
makes the prices lower.

01:17:35.220 --> 01:17:37.350 align:middle line:84%
So, in some sense,
firms should not

01:17:37.350 --> 01:17:40.550 align:middle line:84%
want to bundle in
a two-firm model.

01:17:40.550 --> 01:17:43.680 align:middle line:90%


01:17:43.680 --> 01:17:45.960 align:middle line:84%
What's noteworthy, though,
is while the firms don't

01:17:45.960 --> 01:17:47.970 align:middle line:84%
want to bundle,
the consumers also

01:17:47.970 --> 01:17:49.410 align:middle line:90%
don't want the firms to bundle.

01:17:49.410 --> 01:17:53.320 align:middle line:84%
So the prices go down, but
the consumers are worse off.

01:17:53.320 --> 01:17:57.060 align:middle line:84%
And that's because the
worst match quality

01:17:57.060 --> 01:17:58.750 align:middle line:90%
overwhelms the price drop.

01:17:58.750 --> 01:18:01.483 align:middle line:84%
So there's a price
drop, but it also

01:18:01.483 --> 01:18:03.900 align:middle line:84%
makes the consumers worse off
because they get worse match

01:18:03.900 --> 01:18:04.400 align:middle line:90%
quality.

01:18:04.400 --> 01:18:06.317 align:middle line:84%
And it doesn't compensate--
and the price drop

01:18:06.317 --> 01:18:07.775 align:middle line:84%
isn't big enough
to compensate them

01:18:07.775 --> 01:18:09.050 align:middle line:90%
for the drop in match quality.

01:18:09.050 --> 01:18:11.750 align:middle line:90%


01:18:11.750 --> 01:18:14.680 align:middle line:90%
Now, when n equals 2, That's.

01:18:14.680 --> 01:18:15.180 align:middle line:90%
True.

01:18:15.180 --> 01:18:19.010 align:middle line:84%
When n is large, when n
is above some threshold,

01:18:19.010 --> 01:18:22.100 align:middle line:84%
bundling starts to
increase profits.

01:18:22.100 --> 01:18:23.640 align:middle line:90%
And why is that?

01:18:23.640 --> 01:18:26.030 align:middle line:90%
Well, again, the value--

01:18:26.030 --> 01:18:28.850 align:middle line:84%
this sum of epsilon
ijm starts getting

01:18:28.850 --> 01:18:32.630 align:middle line:90%
to be a very tight distribution.

01:18:32.630 --> 01:18:36.260 align:middle line:84%
And so, what's the
density of consumers

01:18:36.260 --> 01:18:41.840 align:middle line:84%
who have very close values for
the best and second best thing?

01:18:41.840 --> 01:18:45.560 align:middle line:84%
That's a density
measured out here.

01:18:45.560 --> 01:18:47.360 align:middle line:84%
If you like me best,
what's the probability

01:18:47.360 --> 01:18:48.277 align:middle line:90%
you like someone else?

01:18:48.277 --> 01:18:51.335 align:middle line:84%
What's the probability the
other person is close to you?

01:18:51.335 --> 01:18:55.740 align:middle line:90%


01:18:55.740 --> 01:19:01.232 align:middle line:84%
When n gets large, holding
m fixed, letting n go large,

01:19:01.232 --> 01:19:02.940 align:middle line:84%
looking further out
in that distribution,

01:19:02.940 --> 01:19:04.620 align:middle line:90%
it gets thinner tailed.

01:19:04.620 --> 01:19:08.100 align:middle line:90%
And the dxj dpj is small.

01:19:08.100 --> 01:19:10.260 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the
p star is high.

01:19:10.260 --> 01:19:15.120 align:middle line:84%
So bundling is good when the
number of firms is large.

01:19:15.120 --> 01:19:17.550 align:middle line:84%
And a third result is when
you fix the number of firms

01:19:17.550 --> 01:19:22.140 align:middle line:84%
and the number of
goods goes to infinity,

01:19:22.140 --> 01:19:25.560 align:middle line:84%
then eventually bundling
drives prices to 0

01:19:25.560 --> 01:19:27.160 align:middle line:90%
as m is going to infinity.

01:19:27.160 --> 01:19:31.180 align:middle line:90%


01:19:31.180 --> 01:19:35.470 align:middle line:84%
So I think in some ways I
wanted to do it because I wanted

01:19:35.470 --> 01:19:36.600 align:middle line:90%
to cover some recent paper.

01:19:36.600 --> 01:19:40.850 align:middle line:84%
And it's a nice example of
how that basic Perloff-Salop

01:19:40.850 --> 01:19:43.550 align:middle line:84%
intuition of price is
about differentiation.

01:19:43.550 --> 01:19:49.122 align:middle line:84%
And it's about dxj dpj is a very
good way to think about a lot--

01:19:49.122 --> 01:19:51.080 align:middle line:84%
it's a good way to think
about a lot of issues,

01:19:51.080 --> 01:19:52.225 align:middle line:90%
bundling included.

01:19:52.225 --> 01:19:55.880 align:middle line:90%


01:19:55.880 --> 01:20:03.750 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I don't understand
why 4 is a result.

01:20:03.750 --> 01:20:05.490 align:middle line:90%
GLENN ELLISON: So--

01:20:05.490 --> 01:20:09.030 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Because we're
just putting infinity

01:20:09.030 --> 01:20:11.370 align:middle line:90%
in the denominator.

01:20:11.370 --> 01:20:13.700 align:middle line:84%
Would they ever charge
an infinite price?

01:20:13.700 --> 01:20:16.060 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: Sorry, I
should have said-- sorry,

01:20:16.060 --> 01:20:19.860 align:middle line:90%
I should have said this is--

01:20:19.860 --> 01:20:22.360 align:middle line:90%
that's with c equals 0.

01:20:22.360 --> 01:20:24.060 align:middle line:90%
So it's going to be that--

01:20:24.060 --> 01:20:26.440 align:middle line:84%
I think I had a c
equals 0 assumption.

01:20:26.440 --> 01:20:29.370 align:middle line:84%
So it's the limit
as-- it probably

01:20:29.370 --> 01:20:36.450 align:middle line:84%
should have written the limit
as n goes to infinity pb over

01:20:36.450 --> 01:20:39.185 align:middle line:90%
m minus c goes to 0.

01:20:39.185 --> 01:20:42.780 align:middle line:90%


01:20:42.780 --> 01:20:47.762 align:middle line:84%
I think maybe I had c equals
0 throughout the paper.

01:20:47.762 --> 01:20:50.540 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:20:50.540 --> 01:20:51.940 align:middle line:90%
OK?

01:20:51.940 --> 01:20:54.860 align:middle line:90%
OK, let me end there then.

01:20:54.860 --> 01:20:57.400 align:middle line:84%
I didn't think I would get
to these last slides, but--

01:20:57.400 --> 01:20:58.900 align:middle line:84%
so anyway, on Monday,
what I'm going

01:20:58.900 --> 01:21:04.600 align:middle line:84%
to do is discuss oligopoly price
discrimination, where you have

01:21:04.600 --> 01:21:07.210 align:middle line:84%
oligopolies who are competing
to sell multiple products,

01:21:07.210 --> 01:21:08.920 align:middle line:84%
and therefore, are going
to be price discriminating

01:21:08.920 --> 01:21:10.003 align:middle line:90%
selling multiple products.

01:21:10.003 --> 01:21:12.670 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm going to do two
empirical papers, Bresnahan

01:21:12.670 --> 01:21:14.320 align:middle line:90%
and Miller-Weinberg.

01:21:14.320 --> 01:21:19.090 align:middle line:84%
Bresnahan, I took the star off
it this year because Bresnahan,

01:21:19.090 --> 01:21:20.270 align:middle line:90%
it's original.

01:21:20.270 --> 01:21:22.480 align:middle line:90%
It's a real classic.

01:21:22.480 --> 01:21:25.120 align:middle line:84%
It really looks 40 years
old when you read it.

01:21:25.120 --> 01:21:28.180 align:middle line:84%
And you're like, wow, I can't
believe you do things that way.

01:21:28.180 --> 01:21:30.460 align:middle line:84%
So I'll discuss the
ideas in Bresnahan

01:21:30.460 --> 01:21:33.580 align:middle line:84%
not going into the details,
which now look very old

01:21:33.580 --> 01:21:34.120 align:middle line:90%
fashioned.

01:21:34.120 --> 01:21:36.040 align:middle line:84%
And then I'll discuss
Miller-Weinberg.

01:21:36.040 --> 01:21:38.410 align:middle line:84%
And I'm doing less
empirical work

01:21:38.410 --> 01:21:41.488 align:middle line:84%
here because Tobias is talking
about demand estimation here.

01:21:41.488 --> 01:21:43.030 align:middle line:84%
And an awful lot of
demand estimation

01:21:43.030 --> 01:21:45.850 align:middle line:84%
is really about estimating
static models of product

01:21:45.850 --> 01:21:48.880 align:middle line:84%
competition with those
epsilon ij kinds of models

01:21:48.880 --> 01:21:51.780 align:middle line:90%
that I talked about.

01:21:51.780 --> 01:22:11.000 align:middle line:90%